-- Q1 LYME DISEASE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 42-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 10-day history of a slowly expanding rash on her right thigh. She recalls a camping trip to the New Forest 3 weeks ago. The rash is circular, approximately 12 cm in diameter, with central clearing and an outer red border — a bull''s-eye appearance. She has mild fatigue and intermittent arthralgia. She has no fever. Her GP suspects Lyme disease. According to NICE guidance on Lyme disease, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Perform a blood test for Borrelia burgdorferi antibodies and treat only if positive',
'Prescribe doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days without waiting for serological confirmation; the rash (erythema migrans) is diagnostic of Lyme disease and treatment should not be delayed',
'Refer urgently to infectious diseases as Lyme disease always requires inpatient IV antibiotic therapy',
'Reassure the patient and monitor the rash for a further 2 weeks before considering antibiotics',
'Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 7 days as this is adequate for early Lyme disease',
'B',
'CORRECT. Erythema migrans (EM) is pathognomonic of Lyme disease — the diagnosis is clinical and serological testing is NOT required before starting treatment. NICE guidance on Lyme disease states: if erythema migrans is present, start antibiotic therapy immediately without waiting for serology. Serology is unreliable in early Lyme disease (antibodies may not yet have developed). First-line treatment: doxycycline 100 mg BD for 21 days (or amoxicillin 500 mg TDS if doxycycline is contraindicated — pregnancy, children under 12). Note: a 7-day course of amoxicillin (option E) is too short — NICE recommends 21 days for doxycycline or amoxicillin. Longer courses are used for disseminated Lyme disease (neurological or cardiac involvement). NICE',
'Incorrect. Serological testing for Lyme disease should not delay treatment when erythema migrans is present. The EM rash is diagnostic and NICE guidance specifically states that antibiotics should be started without waiting for test results. Additionally, serology may be falsely negative in early Lyme disease as antibodies take 2–4 weeks to develop after infection.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Most patients with early Lyme disease (EM alone, no neurological or cardiac involvement) are treated with oral antibiotics in primary care. IV antibiotic therapy (IV ceftriaxone) is reserved for disseminated Lyme disease with neurological involvement (Lyme neuroborreliosis — radiculopathy, facial palsy, meningitis) or Lyme carditis. Routine ID referral for uncomplicated EM is not necessary.',
'Incorrect. A 2-week monitoring period before starting antibiotics is inappropriate when erythema migrans is present — the diagnosis is made and treatment should begin immediately. Delay risks progression to disseminated Lyme disease with neurological, cardiac, or musculoskeletal complications.',
'Incorrect. Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS is an appropriate alternative antibiotic for Lyme disease (when doxycycline is contraindicated), but the duration of 7 days is insufficient. NICE recommends 21 days of amoxicillin for EM. A 7-day course carries a high risk of treatment failure and progression to later stages of Lyme disease.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q2 SEPSIS RECOGNITION IN PRIMARY CARE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 74-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease (eGFR 42 mL/min) is visited at home by his GP following a call from his wife. He has had a 2-day history of worsening confusion, dysuria, and rigors. His wife reports he has been increasingly unwell and not eating. On examination: temperature 38.8°C, HR 118 bpm, RR 24/min, BP 94/60 mmHg, SpO2 93% on air. He is confused (GCS 13/15). His urinary catheter is in situ and is draining cloudy, malodorous urine. According to NICE guidance on sepsis recognition and management (NG51), which of the following most accurately identifies his risk category and the most appropriate immediate action?',
'This is moderate sepsis; prescribe oral co-amoxiclav and arrange a urine culture; review in 24 hours',
'This is high-risk sepsis with features of life-threatening organ dysfunction; call 999 immediately for emergency hospital transfer; do not delay for investigations or oral antibiotics in primary care',
'This is a urinary tract infection in a diabetic patient; prescribe oral trimethoprim and arrange GP review in 48 hours',
'This is acute kidney injury from dehydration; recommend oral fluids and arrange bloods in 24 hours',
'This is possible urosepsis; start IV antibiotics in primary care and transfer to hospital when the first dose has been administered',
'B',
'CORRECT. This patient meets high-risk sepsis criteria per NICE NG51. NICE defines high-risk features (Red Flag Sepsis) as any ONE of: altered mental status (GCS <15 — present, GCS 13), respiratory rate ≥25/min (present, RR 24 — borderline), systolic BP ≤90 mmHg (present, BP 94/60), SpO2 <92% (present, 93% — borderline but concerning in context), non-blanching rash, or lactate ≥2 mmol/L. This patient has: confusion, hypotension (BP 94/60), tachycardia (HR 118), fever, and possible urosepsis in an immunocompromised host (diabetes, CKD). This is a medical emergency. The Sepsis Six bundle in hospital begins within 1 hour: blood cultures, IV antibiotics, IV fluids (fluid challenge), urine output monitoring, lactate measurement, and high-flow oxygen. In primary care, the GP should call 999 immediately without delay for investigations or oral antibiotics — every hour of delay in treating sepsis significantly increases mortality. NICE',
'Incorrect. This is NOT moderate sepsis — the patient has hypotension (BP 94/60), altered consciousness, and multiple organ dysfunction markers. Oral co-amoxiclav and 24-hour review would be dangerously inadequate. This patient needs immediate 999 activation for emergency transfer and will require IV antibiotics, IV fluids, and intensive monitoring in hospital.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. A simple UTI does not cause hypotension, confusion, and systemic septic features. Oral trimethoprim is for uncomplicated lower UTI in otherwise well patients. This patient has systemic sepsis with haemodynamic compromise — prescribing oral trimethoprim and reviewing in 48 hours would be a life-threatening management error.',
'Incorrect. While CKD patients are at risk of AKT from dehydration, this patient''s clinical picture — fever, confusion, hypotension, tachycardia, and catheter with cloudy malodorous urine — is septic shock from urosepsis, not simple dehydration. Oral fluids and delayed bloods are entirely inadequate. Emergency transfer is required.',
'Incorrect. Starting IV antibiotics in primary care before transfer significantly delays emergency hospital care. GPs rarely have IV antibiotic access at home visits, and any delay in transfer for IV antibiotic administration risks worsening haemodynamic compromise. The correct action is immediate 999 activation — the hospital will deliver IV antibiotics within the 1-hour target. If IV access is available and transfer will be significantly delayed, one dose of IV amoxicillin can be given, but this should not delay calling 999.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q3 HIV TESTING IN PRIMARY CARE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 28-year-old man presents to his GP requesting an HIV test. He reports unprotected anal intercourse with a new male partner 6 weeks ago. He has no symptoms. He is not on PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis). His GP considers testing options. According to NICE guidance on HIV testing and BASHH guidelines, which of the following most accurately describes the recommended testing approach and the window period for HIV testing?',
'A rapid HIV point-of-care test is the most accurate method at 6 weeks post-exposure and a negative result can definitively exclude HIV infection',
'An HIV combination (4th generation) antigen/antibody test at 6 weeks post-exposure can give a conclusive negative result; if negative, a repeat test at 12 weeks is no longer required with 4th generation assays',
'An HIV antibody-only test at 6 weeks is conclusive; antigen testing is not required in primary care',
'HIV testing should be deferred until 3 months post-exposure as no test is reliable before this point',
'Perform an HIV test now; if negative, prescribe post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) as a precaution',
'B',
'CORRECT. Fourth-generation HIV tests (combination antigen/antibody assays) detect both HIV p24 antigen and HIV antibodies. The p24 antigen appears approximately 2–4 weeks after infection (before antibodies develop), giving 4th generation tests a shorter window period than antibody-only tests. BASHH and NICE guidance: a 4th generation HIV test at ≥45 days (approximately 6 weeks) post-exposure gives a conclusive negative result — no repeat at 12 weeks is needed if this is negative. A test at 6 weeks post-exposure using a 4th generation assay is therefore appropriate and conclusive for this patient. Important distinction: rapid point-of-care tests (option A) may be antibody-only (3rd generation) with a longer window period — not all POCTs are 4th generation, so a negative POCT may not be conclusive at 6 weeks. The 4th generation laboratory test is the recommended confirmatory test. NICE/BASHH',
'Incorrect. Not all rapid HIV point-of-care tests (POCTs) are 4th generation — some are antibody-only (3rd generation) with a window period of up to 12 weeks. A negative POCT at 6 weeks may not be conclusive if it does not include p24 antigen detection. The 4th generation laboratory test is recommended for conclusive results. However, if the POCT is confirmed to be 4th generation, a negative result at 45+ days is conclusive.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. HIV antibody-only tests (3rd generation) have a window period of up to 12 weeks — they are not conclusive at 6 weeks. The introduction of 4th generation combination antigen/antibody tests shortened the window period significantly. Using an antibody-only test at 6 weeks and claiming it is conclusive would be incorrect.',
'Incorrect. HIV testing with 4th generation assays can give a conclusive result at 45 days (approximately 6 weeks) post-exposure — deferring to 3 months is no longer necessary with modern 4th generation testing. This recommendation is outdated and would unnecessarily delay the patient receiving a conclusive result.',
'Incorrect. PEP (post-exposure prophylaxis) must be started within 72 hours of exposure — this patient is 6 weeks post-exposure, which is far beyond the 72-hour PEP window. PEP is ineffective after 72 hours. The appropriate management is 4th generation HIV testing at this point, not PEP prescription.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q4 TUBERCULOSIS CONTACT TRACING
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP is notified by the local health protection team that one of their patients — a 34-year-old man — has been confirmed to have smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis. He is a primary school teacher who has been symptomatic for approximately 3 months before diagnosis. He lives with his wife and two children aged 7 and 4. According to NICE guidance on tuberculosis (NG33) and public health responsibilities, which of the following most accurately describes the contact tracing obligations and the management of his close contacts?',
'Only the wife and children require testing; the school contacts do not require screening as children rarely transmit TB to each other',
'The household contacts (wife and children) and school contacts (children and staff who had prolonged exposure) all require contact tracing and screening; the GP should liaise with the health protection team who coordinate this; household contacts under 5 (the 4-year-old) should receive BCG vaccination and isoniazid prophylaxis regardless of test result',
'Only smear-negative contacts need testing; smear-positive contacts are automatically treated',
'Contact tracing is not required as the patient is now on treatment and is no longer infectious after 2 weeks of therapy',
'Household contacts should self-refer to a chest clinic; the GP has no active role in TB contact tracing',
'B',
'CORRECT. NICE NG33 on tuberculosis outlines the contact tracing obligations for smear-positive pulmonary TB. Key points: (1) Smear-positive TB is the most infectious form — close contacts require screening; (2) Household contacts (wife and children) are highest priority — they should have: TST or IGRA (QuantiFERON-TB Gold), chest X-ray, and clinical assessment; (3) School contacts — because this teacher has been symptomatic for 3 months in a primary school, a school outbreak investigation is required by the health protection team; children who shared a classroom with him for extended periods require contact screening; (4) Children under 5 who are close contacts of smear-positive TB: NICE recommends BCG vaccination (if not previously vaccinated) AND isoniazid prophylaxis (chemoprophylaxis) regardless of IGRA result — because they are at highest risk of progression to disease and may have false-negative tests; (5) The GP should liaise with and support the health protection team who coordinate the outbreak investigation. NICE',
'Incorrect. School contacts DO require screening when the index case is a teacher with smear-positive pulmonary TB who has been symptomatic for 3 months. This is a prolonged exposure in an enclosed setting with a vulnerable population (children). The health protection team will determine the extent of school screening based on the exposure assessment.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Contact tracing is determined by the infectious status of the index case and the duration of exposure — not whether the contacts are smear-positive or negative. Contacts need screening to determine if they have been infected with TB. Close contacts with active TB disease or LTBI (latent TB infection) require treatment regardless of their smear status.',
'Incorrect. Patients with smear-positive pulmonary TB are considered non-infectious after approximately 2 weeks of effective treatment (when sputum smear converts to negative). However, contact tracing must begin as soon as the case is confirmed — contacts who were exposed before treatment commenced need screening regardless of the patient''s current infectiousness. The 2-week threshold does not eliminate the need for retrospective contact tracing.',
'Incorrect. The GP plays an active role in TB contact tracing — they are responsible for: informing close household contacts about the need for screening, supporting the health protection team''s investigation, providing medical information about the index case (with consent where appropriate), and managing any contacts who test positive for LTBI or active TB. Self-referral alone is insufficient.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q5 MENINGOCOCCAL DISEASE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP receives a phone call from a worried mother at 10pm. Her 3-year-old daughter has had a high fever for 12 hours and has now developed a rash. The mother describes small red spots that are spreading rapidly over the child''s body and legs. The GP asks her to press a glass against the spots — the spots do not fade under pressure (non-blanching). The child is increasingly drowsy and difficult to rouse. According to NICE guidance on meningococcal disease management in primary care, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate advice?',
'Advise the mother to give calpol and bring the child to the surgery first thing in the morning',
'Advise the mother to call NHS 111 for advice as the symptoms may represent viral illness',
'Advise the mother to call 999 immediately; while awaiting the ambulance, if the GP can attend, administer IM/IV benzylpenicillin 300 mg (for a child aged 1–9 years) before transfer — do not delay 999 for any reason',
'Advise the mother to drive the child to A&E herself as this will be faster than waiting for an ambulance',
'Ask the mother to monitor the rash for a further 2 hours and call back if it spreads',
'C',
'CORRECT. This child has a classic presentation of meningococcal septicaemia — a non-blanching petechial/purpuric rash with fever, rapidly deteriorating consciousness, and spreading lesions. This is a paediatric emergency with very high mortality (15–20%) and morbidity (amputations in 10–15% of survivors from peripheral ischaemia). Management per NICE: (1) Call 999 immediately — do not delay for any reason; (2) If a GP can attend before or during transfer: administer IM/IV benzylpenicillin immediately — this is the pre-hospital treatment for suspected meningococcal disease: Infants (<1 year): 300 mg; Children 1–9 years: 600 mg; Adults and children ≥10 years: 1200 mg. Note: penicillin allergy — use IM cefotaxime or chloramphenicol; (3) High-flow oxygen; (4) Do not delay transfer for any investigations or treatment — speed of transfer is paramount. NICE',
'Incorrect. A non-blanching rash in a febrile child is a medical emergency and must NEVER be told to wait until morning. Meningococcal septicaemia can be fatal within hours. Advising paracetamol and morning surgery attendance would constitute gross negligence.',
'Incorrect. Calling NHS 111 introduces dangerous delay in a life-threatening emergency. Non-blanching rash + fever + deteriorating consciousness = 999 emergency. NHS 111 is not appropriate for suspected meningococcal disease.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While driving to A&E might seem faster, it is potentially dangerous — the child could deteriorate en route without medical support. An ambulance crew can provide emergency care (oxygen, IV access, fluid resuscitation) during transfer. Additionally, if the GP can attend, pre-hospital benzylpenicillin significantly improves outcome. 999 is the correct call.',
'Incorrect. Monitoring a spreading non-blanching rash for a further 2 hours in a child with deteriorating consciousness is completely inappropriate. Purpuric lesions in meningococcal disease represent dermal haemorrhage from septic emboli and DIC — they are not a watch-and-wait phenomenon. Every minute of delay worsens outcome.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q6 GONORRHOEA AND STI MANAGEMENT
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 24-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 5-day history of vaginal discharge described as yellow-green and purulent, with lower abdominal pain and dyspareunia. She has a new sexual partner of 2 months. She has not used barrier contraception. On examination, she has cervical motion tenderness and mucopurulent cervical discharge on speculum examination. Her temperature is 37.7°C. According to NICE guidance on pelvic inflammatory disease and BASHH STI guidelines, which of the following most accurately describes the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe oral metronidazole 400 mg BD for 7 days and review in 1 week',
'Take endocervical swabs for culture and NAAT testing; treat empirically for PID without waiting for results — oral ofloxacin 400 mg BD plus oral metronidazole 400 mg BD for 14 days is first-line for outpatient PID treatment; advise no sexual intercourse until both patient and partner are treated',
'Refer urgently to gynaecology for laparoscopy to confirm the diagnosis before starting antibiotics',
'Prescribe oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days and advise the partner to attend his GP',
'Send swabs and await results before prescribing antibiotics as empirical treatment may mask the causative organism',
'B',
'CORRECT. This presentation is consistent with pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) — infection of the upper genital tract (uterus, fallopian tubes, ovaries). Diagnosis is clinical (cervical motion tenderness + mucopurulent discharge + lower abdominal pain in a sexually active woman). NICE and BASHH guidelines on PID: (1) Swabs should be taken (high vaginal swab and endocervical NAAT for gonorrhoea and chlamydia) but results should NOT be awaited before starting treatment — empirical therapy is essential; (2) Outpatient treatment: oral ofloxacin 400 mg BD + oral metronidazole 400 mg BD for 14 days covers N. gonorrhoeae, C. trachomatis, and anaerobes; (3) Inpatient IV treatment is needed for: severe illness, pregnancy, no response to oral treatment, or surgical emergency cannot be excluded; (4) Partner notification and treatment is essential; (5) Advise abstinence until both partners complete treatment. NICE/BASHH',
'Incorrect. Metronidazole alone does not cover Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis — the two most common causes of PID. Single-agent metronidazole for PID is inadequate and risks treatment failure with progression to complications (tubo-ovarian abscess, infertility, ectopic pregnancy, chronic pelvic pain).',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Laparoscopy is the gold standard for definitive diagnosis of PID but is not routinely required before treatment. BASHH and NICE support empirical treatment based on clinical criteria. Laparoscopy is considered if the diagnosis is uncertain, if the patient fails to respond to outpatient treatment, or if a tubo-ovarian abscess needs drainage.',
'Incorrect. Doxycycline alone for 7 days is insufficient for PID — it does not cover Neisseria gonorrhoeae adequately at this dose/duration, and the duration is too short. BASHH recommends 14-day courses for PID. Advising the partner to see his GP without a partner notification service referral and specific treatment instruction is also inadequate.',
'Incorrect. Awaiting swab results before treating PID introduces dangerous delay. PID can cause irreversible fallopian tube damage leading to infertility with every day of untreated infection. NICE and BASHH are explicit: start empirical treatment immediately when PID is clinically suspected, regardless of microbiological results.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q7 INFLUENZA AND ANTIVIRALS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 68-year-old woman with COPD and type 2 diabetes presents to her GP in January with a 36-hour history of sudden-onset fever (39.1°C), severe myalgia, headache, dry cough, and profound fatigue. She had her influenza vaccination in October. Her oxygen saturation is 94% on air. She has no rash. Her GP suspects influenza. According to NICE guidance on antiviral treatment for influenza (NG33 update), which of the following most accurately describes the role of antiviral therapy in this patient?',
'Antivirals are not indicated as she has been vaccinated against influenza this season',
'Antiviral therapy is not beneficial as more than 24 hours have elapsed since symptom onset',
'Offer oseltamivir (Tamiflu) 75 mg BD for 5 days within 48 hours of symptom onset; she is in a high-risk group (COPD, diabetes, age ≥65) and antivirals reduce the duration and severity of influenza and the risk of complications including pneumonia',
'Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 5 days as secondary bacterial pneumonia is the expected complication',
'Admit to hospital immediately as influenza in a diabetic patient with COPD always requires inpatient treatment',
'C',
'CORRECT. NICE guidance on antiviral treatment for influenza recommends oseltamivir (or zanamivir) for at-risk groups when influenza is circulating in the community and the patient presents within 48 hours of symptom onset. At-risk groups include: age ≥65, chronic respiratory disease (COPD), diabetes mellitus, chronic heart/renal/liver disease, immunosuppression, BMI >40, and pregnancy. This patient qualifies on three counts (age 65+, COPD, diabetes). She is within the 48-hour window (36 hours). Vaccination reduces but does not eliminate influenza risk — vaccinated patients in at-risk groups should still receive antivirals if they develop influenza within 48 hours. Oseltamivir: 75 mg BD for 5 days (renally adjusted if eGFR reduced). Antivirals reduce: duration of illness by approximately 1 day, hospitalisation risk, and risk of serious complications (pneumonia, acute exacerbation of COPD). NICE',
'Incorrect. Vaccination reduces the risk of influenza but does not provide complete protection — vaccine effectiveness varies by season (typically 40–60%). Vaccinated patients in high-risk groups who develop clinical influenza within 48 hours of symptom onset should still receive antiviral treatment. Vaccination status does not exclude the benefit of antiviral therapy.',
'Incorrect. The 48-hour window from symptom onset applies — this patient is at 36 hours, which is within the treatment window. NICE guidance recommends antivirals up to 48 hours after symptom onset in at-risk patients. After 48 hours, oseltamivir can still be considered if the patient is severely ill or at very high risk, but the benefit is reduced.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Amoxicillin treats secondary bacterial pneumonia — it has no activity against influenza virus. Prescribing antibiotics for uncomplicated influenza is inappropriate and contributes to antibiotic resistance. Antibiotics should only be added if there is evidence of secondary bacterial infection (persistent fever beyond 5 days, new localised consolidation on CXR, purulent sputum).',
'Incorrect. Most high-risk patients with influenza can be managed at home with antivirals, analgesia, antipyretics, and adequate hydration. Hospital admission is appropriate if there is: SpO2 <92%, haemodynamic compromise, acute exacerbation of COPD requiring nebulised bronchodilators or steroids, or secondary pneumonia. This patient has SpO2 94% and is not in severe respiratory distress — home management with oseltamivir is appropriate, with clear safety-netting advice.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q8 MALARIA PROPHYLAXIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 32-year-old woman who is 14 weeks pregnant attends her GP. She is planning to travel to rural sub-Saharan Africa (Kenya) for a 3-week business trip in 4 weeks'' time. She is generally well with no significant medical history. She asks about malaria prophylaxis. According to current PHE/NICE guidance on malaria prevention in pregnancy, which of the following most accurately represents the recommended approach?',
'Prescribe chloroquine as it is the safest antimalarial in pregnancy and effective against Plasmodium falciparum in Kenya',
'Prescribe doxycycline 100 mg OD as it is safe in the second trimester and provides effective prophylaxis against Plasmodium falciparum',
'Advise strongly against non-essential travel to malaria-endemic areas in pregnancy; if travel is unavoidable, prescribe mefloquine (Lariam) as it is safe in the second trimester; ensure insect avoidance measures (DEET, mosquito nets)',
'Advise strongly against non-essential travel to a high-risk malaria zone during pregnancy if possible; if travel is unavoidable, prescribe atovaquone-proguanil (Malarone) as it is the safest option in the second trimester',
'No prophylaxis is needed as Plasmodium falciparum malaria is rare in tourists staying in hotels in Kenya',
'C',
'CORRECT. Malaria in pregnancy is extremely dangerous — it causes maternal anaemia, placental malaria, preterm delivery, intrauterine growth restriction, and maternal death. Pregnant women are 3× more likely to develop severe malaria than non-pregnant adults. PHE and NICE/TRAVAX guidance: (1) Advise against non-essential travel to high-risk malaria zones (sub-Saharan Africa, P. falciparum endemic) during pregnancy; (2) If travel is unavoidable: Mefloquine (Lariam) is safe in the second and third trimesters (NOT first trimester — avoid <13 weeks). It is effective against P. falciparum and is the recommended prophylaxis for pregnant women travelling to high-risk areas when chloroquine resistance is prevalent. This patient is at 14 weeks — second trimester — so mefloquine is appropriate; (3) Chloroquine (option A): ineffective in Kenya as P. falciparum is chloroquine-resistant throughout sub-Saharan Africa; (4) Doxycycline (option B): CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy (causes foetal bone/tooth damage); (5) Atovaquone-proguanil (option D): safety data in pregnancy is limited — not recommended in pregnancy. PHE/NICE',
'Incorrect. Chloroquine is ineffective in Kenya and most of sub-Saharan Africa because P. falciparum (the predominant and most lethal species) is universally resistant to chloroquine in this region. Prescribing chloroquine for malaria prophylaxis in Kenya would provide no protection against the most dangerous form of malaria.',
'Incorrect. Doxycycline is absolutely CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy. It causes permanent discolouration of foetal teeth and inhibits foetal bone development. Doxycycline should never be prescribed for malaria prophylaxis during any trimester of pregnancy.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Atovaquone-proguanil (Malarone) has limited safety data in pregnancy. TRAVAX and PHE do not recommend it as first-line in pregnancy due to insufficient evidence of safety, particularly for the proguanil component which is a folate antagonist. Mefloquine in the second and third trimesters has a better-established safety profile for malaria prophylaxis in pregnancy.',
'Incorrect. Rural sub-Saharan Africa carries one of the highest malaria transmission risks in the world — it is not a low-risk destination for tourists. P. falciparum (the most lethal species) is endemic throughout Kenya. The risk is not eliminated by hotel accommodation — malaria transmission occurs outdoors and in non-air-conditioned indoor environments. Claiming prophylaxis is unnecessary is factually incorrect and potentially fatal.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q9 CHICKENPOX IN PREGNANCY
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 26-year-old woman who is 22 weeks pregnant presents to her GP with a 2-day history of fever and a widespread itchy rash. On examination, she has vesicular lesions in various stages of development (some crusted, some fresh blisters, some papules) distributed across her trunk, face, and limbs. She has no previous history of chickenpox and has not been vaccinated. She is unsure if her 4-year-old son had chickenpox last week. According to NICE guidance on chickenpox in pregnancy, which of the following represents the most complete and appropriate management?',
'Reassure her that chickenpox is mild in adults and prescribe chlorphenamine for the itch',
'Prescribe oral aciclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7 days; refer urgently to the maternal medicine or obstetric team; advise her to avoid contact with other pregnant women and immunocompromised individuals until all lesions have crusted; check varicella IgG status in the 4-year-old''s contacts',
'Prescribe varicella-zoster immunoglobulin (VZIG) as this is the treatment for active chickenpox in pregnancy',
'Advise her to attend the emergency department for IV aciclovir as all chickenpox in pregnancy requires inpatient treatment',
'Arrange varicella IgG serology before prescribing antivirals as the diagnosis must be confirmed serologically first',
'B',
'CORRECT. Chickenpox (primary varicella) in pregnancy carries significant risks: (1) Severe maternal disease — pneumonitis (occurs in 10–20% of pregnant women with chickenpox), hepatitis, encephalitis; maternal mortality is higher than in non-pregnant adults; (2) Foetal/neonatal risk — congenital varicella syndrome (skin scarring, limb hypoplasia, eye defects, neurological abnormalities) if infection occurs before 28 weeks (risk approximately 2% between 13–20 weeks); neonatal varicella if maternal infection occurs within 5 days of delivery. Management per NICE: (1) Oral aciclovir 800 mg 5× daily for 7 days (should be started within 24 hours of rash onset for maximum benefit) — safe in pregnancy; (2) Urgent obstetric/maternal medicine referral for monitoring; (3) Infection control — avoid susceptible pregnant women and immunocompromised individuals; (4) VZIG: used for PROPHYLAXIS in susceptible exposed pregnant women — NOT as treatment for active disease. NICE',
'Incorrect. Chickenpox in pregnancy is NOT mild — it carries a risk of varicella pneumonitis (10–20%), which can be fatal. Chlorphenamine for itch without antiviral treatment and obstetric referral is completely inadequate management for chickenpox in pregnancy.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. VZIG (varicella-zoster immunoglobulin) is used for POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS in susceptible pregnant women (those without immunity) who have been exposed to varicella — to prevent or attenuate the disease. It is NOT a treatment for active chickenpox — once the rash has appeared, VZIG has no role. The treatment is aciclovir.',
'Incorrect. Most cases of chickenpox in pregnancy can be managed with oral aciclovir in the community, combined with urgent obstetric referral. IV aciclovir and inpatient admission are required for: varicella pneumonitis (cough, breathlessness, hypoxia), encephalitis, severe hepatitis, or if the patient cannot tolerate oral medication. This patient has a rash only — oral management with urgent obstetric referral is appropriate.',
'Incorrect. The diagnosis of chickenpox is clinical — based on the characteristic polymorphic vesicular rash (lesions in various stages: macules, papules, vesicles, and crusted lesions simultaneously) with fever. Serological confirmation before starting treatment would delay antiviral therapy that is most effective when started within 24 hours of rash onset. Treatment should not be delayed for serology.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q10 CLOSTRIDIUM DIFFICILE PREVENTION
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 78-year-old woman with a history of two previous Clostridioides difficile infections (the last one 8 months ago) attends her GP with a 4-day history of lower urinary tract infection symptoms. Her urine dipstick shows nitrites and leucocytes. Her urine culture grows E. coli sensitive to trimethoprim, nitrofurantoin, and ciprofloxacin. Her eGFR is 48 mL/min/1.73m². She is currently not on any antibiotics. According to NICE guidance on antimicrobial prescribing and C. difficile risk reduction, which of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic choice for her UTI?',
'Ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD for 7 days as fluoroquinolones have the lowest risk of C. difficile recurrence',
'Trimethoprim 200 mg BD for 7 days as this is standard first-line therapy for lower UTI in women',
'Nitrofurantoin 100 mg modified-release BD for 5 days is the appropriate choice — her eGFR of 48 mL/min is above the threshold for nitrofurantoin use (contraindicated below eGFR 30 mL/min per NICE); nitrofurantoin has a lower C. difficile risk than broad-spectrum agents',
'Prescribe no antibiotics; treat with cranberry juice and increased fluid intake as she is high risk for C. difficile',
'Co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 7 days to ensure broad-spectrum coverage given her high-risk status',
'C',
'CORRECT. Antibiotic choice in a patient with previous C. difficile infection must minimise the risk of triggering C. difficile recurrence. C. difficile risk ranking (highest to lowest): clindamycin > fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) > broad-spectrum penicillins/cephalosporins > narrow-spectrum agents (trimethoprim, nitrofurantoin). Ciprofloxacin (option A) carries the HIGHEST C. difficile recurrence risk of the options listed. Co-amoxiclav (option E) is also high-risk. Nitrofurantoin has a low C. difficile risk (concentrated in the urinary tract with minimal colonic disruption) and is effective for uncomplicated lower UTI. Regarding eGFR: nitrofurantoin is contraindicated below eGFR 30 mL/min (ineffective — insufficient urinary concentration). At eGFR 48, nitrofurantoin is appropriate. NICE antimicrobial guidelines and C. difficile risk guidance support nitrofurantoin as the preferred choice in this patient. NICE',
'Incorrect. Ciprofloxacin (a fluoroquinolone) carries one of the highest risks of C. difficile colitis of any antibiotic class. In a patient with two previous C. difficile infections, prescribing ciprofloxacin would be inappropriate and potentially life-threatening. NICE guidance specifically cautions against unnecessary fluoroquinolone use in patients at high C. difficile risk.',
'Incorrect. While trimethoprim is first-line for uncomplicated lower UTI in most women per NICE antimicrobial guidelines, in a patient with recurrent C. difficile infection, the C. difficile risk must be considered in antibiotic selection. Trimethoprim has a moderate C. difficile risk (higher than nitrofurantoin). However, it is sensitive on culture and preferable to fluoroquinolones and co-amoxiclav. In practice, nitrofurantoin at an eGFR of 48 is a better choice in this high-risk patient.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. No treatment is not appropriate for a confirmed UTI with symptoms and positive culture. Withholding antibiotics risks progression to pyelonephritis and systemic sepsis, particularly in an elderly woman. Cranberry juice has no evidence base for treating established UTI.',
'Incorrect. Co-amoxiclav (amoxicillin-clavulanate) is a broad-spectrum antibiotic with a significant risk of C. difficile colitis — significantly higher than nitrofurantoin. It is not appropriate as first-line for uncomplicated lower UTI per NICE guidelines and is particularly inappropriate in a patient with recurrent C. difficile history.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q11 NOTIFIABLE DISEASES
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP diagnoses a case of suspected typhoid fever in a 28-year-old man recently returned from Bangladesh. The GP is unsure about the notification requirements. According to the Health Protection (Notification) Regulations 2010 in England, which of the following most accurately describes the legal notification requirements for this case?',
'Notification is voluntary for typhoid fever; GPs can choose whether to report at their discretion',
'Typhoid fever is a notifiable disease; the GP must notify the local health protection team (UKHSA — UK Health Security Agency) by telephone within 24 hours of suspicion (before laboratory confirmation), followed by written notification within 3 days',
'Only laboratory-confirmed cases of typhoid fever need to be notified — clinical suspicion alone does not trigger notification',
'Notification is only required if the patient is a food handler or healthcare worker',
'Notification is made only to the treating hospital specialist, not to public health authorities',
'B',
'CORRECT. Under the Health Protection (Notification) Regulations 2010 (England), registered medical practitioners (including GPs) have a legal duty to notify specified notifiable diseases to the designated officer at the local authority/UKHSA (UK Health Security Agency, previously PHE). Typhoid fever (Salmonella typhi) is a notifiable disease. Key points: (1) Notification is on clinical suspicion — laboratory confirmation is NOT required before notifying; (2) Telephone/verbal notification to the local health protection team within 24 hours of suspicion; (3) Written notification (completion of notification form) within 3 days; (4) Notification is mandatory — not voluntary — and applies regardless of the patient''s occupation; (5) Other notifiable diseases include: meningococcal disease, tuberculosis, cholera, measles, mumps, rubella, scarlet fever, whooping cough, Ebola, plague, anthrax, food poisoning. NICE/UKHSA',
'Incorrect. Notification of typhoid fever is NOT voluntary — it is a statutory legal requirement under the Health Protection (Notification) Regulations 2010. Failure to notify is an offence. GPs do not have discretion about whether to report notifiable diseases.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Notification is required on clinical suspicion — laboratory confirmation is not a prerequisite. Waiting for laboratory confirmation before notifying would introduce an unacceptable delay in initiating public health investigation, contact tracing, and source identification. The notification must be made as soon as the GP suspects the condition.',
'Incorrect. The notification obligation applies to all cases of notifiable diseases — not just those in food handlers or healthcare workers. However, if the patient IS a food handler or healthcare worker, additional urgent public health action is required (immediate exclusion from work pending microbiological clearance).',
'Incorrect. Notification of notifiable diseases is made to the local authority designated officer/UKHSA health protection team — not to the treating hospital specialist. The specialist team will receive the clinical information as part of the referral process, but the statutory public health notification is a separate obligation to the public health authorities.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q12 INFECTIVE ENDOCARDITIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 55-year-old man with a known bicuspid aortic valve presents to his GP with a 3-week history of low-grade fever (37.8–38.2°C), malaise, night sweats, and new-onset arthralgia. He had a dental procedure 6 weeks ago. On examination, his temperature is 38.0°C, he has a new harsh systolic murmur at the right upper sternal edge (not previously documented), and his spleen is mildly enlarged. He has multiple small, non-tender, flat reddish lesions on his palms and soles. His CRP is 84 mg/L and ESR 68 mm/hr. His FBC shows a normochromic normocytic anaemia (Hb 108 g/L). Urine dipstick shows 2+ blood (microscopic haematuria). According to your knowledge of infective endocarditis, which of the following findings is NOT a recognised major Duke Criterion for infective endocarditis?',
'Positive blood cultures with typical microorganisms (e.g., Streptococcus viridans, Staphylococcus aureus)',
'Evidence of endocardial involvement on echocardiography (oscillating intracardiac mass, abscess, new valve regurgitation)',
'New-onset clubbing of the fingers',
'A new valvular regurgitation murmur on clinical examination',
'Positive blood cultures from two separate samples taken 12 hours apart',
'C',
'CORRECT — ODD ONE OUT. Finger clubbing is a well-recognised MINOR sign of infective endocarditis (IE) but it is NOT a major Duke Criterion. The Duke Criteria for IE include: Major Criteria: (1) Positive blood cultures — typical organisms (Strep viridans, Strep bovis, HACEK group, Staph aureus, or Enterococcus) from ≥2 separate blood cultures, OR persistently positive cultures ≥12 hours apart; (2) Evidence of endocardial involvement — echocardiographic evidence of oscillating intracardiac mass (vegetation), abscess, or new partial dehiscence of prosthetic valve, OR new valvular regurgitation. Minor Criteria include: predisposing heart condition, IV drug use, fever >38°C, vascular phenomena (Janeway lesions — present in this patient as non-tender palmar/plantar lesions), immunological phenomena (Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis — haematuria present), positive serology. Clubbing is occasionally listed as a chronic feature but is NOT one of the formal Duke Criteria. NICE/ESC guidelines on IE',
'Incorrect as odd one out. Positive blood cultures with typical organisms (Streptococcus viridans is the classic dental procedure-associated organism causing IE) IS a major Duke Criterion — specifically, one of the microbiological major criteria.',
'Incorrect as odd one out. Echocardiographic evidence of endocardial involvement (oscillating mass, abscess, new regurgitation) IS a major Duke Criterion — the echocardiographic major criterion.',
'CORRECT — this is the odd one out. Clubbing is a chronic sign associated with IE but is NOT a formal major Duke Criterion.',
'Incorrect as odd one out. A new valvular regurgitation murmur is part of the echocardiographic major criterion when confirmed on echo — on clinical examination alone it is suggestive but the Duke Criterion specifically refers to echocardiographic/imaging evidence.',
'Incorrect as odd one out. Persistent bacteraemia (two positive blood cultures taken ≥12 hours apart, or 3 of 3 positive within 1 hour) IS a major Duke Criterion for IE — the microbiological evidence criterion.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q13 HERPES ZOSTER (SHINGLES)
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 72-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 2-day history of a burning, painful rash on the left side of her chest. On examination, there is a unilateral vesicular rash in a dermatomal distribution (T5 dermatome) that does not cross the midline. She has severe pain rated 8/10. She is otherwise fit and well, takes no immunosuppressive medications, and her last shingles vaccination was offered but declined. She has no ophthalmic involvement. According to NICE guidance on herpes zoster management, which of the following represents the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical aciclovir cream 5% to the rash four times daily for 5 days and paracetamol for pain',
'Prescribe oral valaciclovir 1000 mg TDS for 7 days and adequate analgesia (WHO analgesic ladder); advise on post-herpetic neuralgia risk and discuss shingles vaccination for the future',
'Reassure her that shingles is self-limiting and prescribe paracetamol only — antivirals are not indicated in immunocompetent adults',
'Admit to hospital for IV aciclovir as all shingles in patients over 65 requires inpatient treatment',
'Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 5 days as secondary bacterial superinfection is inevitable',
'B',
'CORRECT. Herpes zoster (shingles) in an immunocompetent adult ≥50 years of age or with severe rash or pain should be treated with oral antiviral therapy. NICE guidance: start antiviral within 72 hours of rash onset for maximum benefit. Antiviral options: aciclovir 800 mg 5× daily for 7 days, OR valaciclovir 1000 mg TDS for 7 days (preferred — better bioavailability, simpler dosing), OR famciclovir 250 mg TDS for 7 days. Benefits: reduce duration and severity of acute neuritis, and significantly reduce the risk of post-herpetic neuralgia (PHN) — the most common and debilitating complication. Analgesia: follow the WHO analgesic ladder — paracetamol, then weak opioids, then strong opioids; gabapentin or amitriptyline for neuropathic pain. Shingles vaccination: the Shingrix (recombinant zoster vaccine) is available on the NHS for adults aged 70–79 — should be discussed. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical aciclovir cream has no role in herpes zoster management — it is used for labial herpes (cold sores — HSV-1). Systemic oral antivirals are required for shingles. The cream does not penetrate to achieve therapeutic levels needed to suppress VZV replication in the dorsal root ganglion.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Antivirals ARE indicated in adults ≥50 years with shingles when started within 72 hours of rash onset. NICE guidance specifically recommends antiviral treatment for this age group, those with moderate-severe pain, non-truncal involvement (head/neck/limbs), or severe rash. Withholding antivirals increases the risk of post-herpetic neuralgia, which can be severely debilitating.',
'Incorrect. The vast majority of shingles in immunocompetent adults is managed with oral antivirals in primary care. Inpatient IV aciclovir is reserved for: ophthalmic zoster (HZO — to prevent ocular complications), disseminated zoster, immunocompromised patients, or complications (encephalitis, pneumonitis). Age alone (over 65) is not an indication for inpatient IV treatment.',
'Incorrect. Antibiotics are not indicated for uncomplicated herpes zoster. Secondary bacterial superinfection (usually Staphylococcal) can occur but is not inevitable — the risk increases if the patient picks or scratches the lesions. Antibiotics should be prescribed only if there is evidence of secondary bacterial infection (increasing redness, warmth, purulent discharge, worsening despite antivirals).',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q14 ANTIBIOTIC STEWARDSHIP — DELAYED PRESCRIBING
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 35-year-old man presents to his GP with a 4-day history of sore throat, runny nose, cough, and a mild fever (37.6°C). He has no dysphagia, no drooling, no stridor, and no trismus. His FeverPAIN score is 2. On examination, his throat is erythematous without exudate. He is demanding antibiotics and states that he "always" gets a chest infection if his throat is not treated with antibiotics early. According to NICE guidance on acute upper respiratory tract infections and antimicrobial stewardship, which of the following most accurately represents the recommended approach?',
'Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 5 days to prevent secondary bacterial infection and reassure the patient',
'Offer a delayed (back-up) antibiotic prescription to be used only if symptoms significantly worsen or do not improve after 1 week; explain that most sore throats are viral and resolve within 7–10 days; provide written information on managing symptoms',
'Tell the patient firmly that antibiotics are never prescribed for sore throat and he should self-manage with over-the-counter remedies only',
'Refer to ENT urgently as a 4-day sore throat with a FeverPAIN score of 2 requires specialist assessment',
'Prescribe a 3-day course of antibiotics as this is sufficient to prevent secondary complications without prolonged antibiotic exposure',
'B',
'CORRECT. NICE guidance on acute sore throat and antimicrobial stewardship supports a nuanced approach. For a FeverPAIN score of 2 (moderate probability of streptococcal infection, approximately 24–38% chance), NICE recommends: no antibiotic or a delayed (back-up) prescription. A delayed prescription (to be collected only if symptoms worsen or do not improve after 1 week) acknowledges the patient''s concerns, reduces antibiotic use by approximately 25–30%, and maintains the therapeutic relationship. Key points for patient education: (1) Most sore throats (85–95%) are viral and resolve within 7–10 days without antibiotics; (2) Antibiotics do not prevent secondary complications (otitis media, sinusitis, chest infection) in most patients; (3) Evidence-based self-care: paracetamol/ibuprofen, fluids, throat lozenges, rest; (4) Return if: symptoms worsen significantly, dysphagia develops, or fever persists beyond 7 days. NICE',
'Incorrect. Prescribing amoxicillin for a FeverPAIN score of 2 (moderate probability) is not recommended by NICE. The majority of patients at this score level have a viral illness. Routine antibiotic prescription contributes to antimicrobial resistance and provides minimal clinical benefit for viral sore throats.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Telling patients firmly that antibiotics are "never" prescribed is inaccurate (NICE supports immediate antibiotics for FeverPAIN 4–5) and unhelpful for the therapeutic relationship. A shared decision-making approach with a delayed prescription is more effective at managing patient expectations while reducing unnecessary antibiotic use.',
'Incorrect. A FeverPAIN score of 2 with no red flag features (drooling, trismus, stridor, peritonsillar swelling, severe dysphagia) does not require ENT referral. Routine ENT referral for straightforward sore throat would be an inappropriate use of specialist resources.',
'Incorrect. NICE does not recommend a 3-day course of antibiotics for sore throat — the evidence base for 3-day courses in pharyngotonsillitis is weaker than for standard 5–10 day courses, and a short course would be unlikely to eradicate Group A Streptococcus if the infection is bacterial. Moreover, at a FeverPAIN score of 2, the appropriate approach is delayed prescription rather than immediate short-course antibiotics.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q15 MPOX (MONKEYPOX)
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 29-year-old man who has sex with men presents to his GP with a 5-day history of fever, lymphadenopathy, and a rash. He reports multiple sexual partners in the past month. The rash consists of multiple well-defined, firm, deep-seated lesions in various stages (some with central umbilication), distributed on his genitalia, buttocks, and face. Some lesions appear pustular. He returned from a trip to Europe 10 days ago. According to current UKHSA and NICE guidance on mpox (monkeypox), which of the following most accurately describes the appropriate primary care management?',
'Diagnose herpes simplex infection and prescribe oral aciclovir 400 mg TDS for 5 days',
'Diagnose syphilis and arrange TPHA/VDRL serology and prescribe IM benzathine penicillin',
'Suspect mpox and contact the local health protection team (UKHSA) immediately; isolate the patient in a side room if in the surgery; do not send routine swabs; arrange same-day assessment at a specialist sexual health or infectious diseases unit; mpox is a notifiable disease',
'Reassure the patient that this is likely molluscum contagiosum; refer routinely to dermatology',
'Take swabs of the lesions and send to the routine microbiology laboratory; prescribe antivirals empirically',
'C',
'CORRECT. Mpox (formerly monkeypox) emerged as a significant outbreak in the UK and globally from 2022. Key clinical features: fever, lymphadenopathy (distinctive — preceding or concurrent with rash), and a characteristic rash with deep-seated, well-defined, firm pustular/umbilicated lesions in various stages of development (unlike molluscum: deeper, larger, more inflammatory; unlike syphilis: more inflammatory pustular character; unlike chickenpox: deeper, fewer lesions). In men who have sex with men (MSM), the rash frequently involves the anogenital region. Primary care management: (1) Contact UKHSA health protection team immediately; (2) Isolate the patient (side room); (3) Do not attempt to sample lesions in primary care — high-risk biosafety procedures required; (4) Refer to specialist sexual health/infectious diseases unit for formal assessment, testing (PCR swabs under controlled conditions), and management; (5) Mpox is a notifiable disease. Treatment: tecovirimat (TPOXX) for severe cases, under specialist guidance. UKHSA/NICE',
'Incorrect. Herpes simplex typically causes clusters of small, painful, superficial blisters on the genitalia — the lesions are less deep and defined than mpox. The bilateral distribution across genitalia, buttocks, and face, combined with lymphadenopathy and fever preceding the rash, is more consistent with mpox. Aciclovir for HSV would not treat mpox.',
'Incorrect. Syphilis (secondary) causes a widespread maculopapular rash including the palms and soles — the lesions are flat or slightly raised, non-pustular, and non-umbilicated. It does not cause the deep, firm, pustular, umbilicated lesions in various stages described here. TPHA serology is appropriate as a co-infection screen in an MSM patient, but syphilis is not the primary diagnosis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Molluscum contagiosum causes small, pearly, umbilicated papules — they are much smaller, more numerous, and less inflammatory than mpox lesions. Molluscum does not cause fever or lymphadenopathy. This patient has fever, lymphadenopathy, and larger, deeper, more inflammatory lesions — mpox must be excluded urgently.',
'Incorrect. Sending routine swabs of suspected mpox lesions to a standard microbiology laboratory is inappropriate and potentially hazardous. Mpox is classified as a high-consequence infectious disease (HCID) — specimens require specialised containment and processing (Category 3 laboratory). Routine labs are not equipped to handle mpox specimens safely. The patient must be referred to a specialist centre.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q16 UTI IN PREGNANCY
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 28-year-old woman who is 16 weeks pregnant attends her GP for her booking appointment. A routine mid-stream urine (MSU) sent at her booking appointment at 10 weeks grew >100,000 colony-forming units/mL of E. coli sensitive to nitrofurantoin, amoxicillin, and cefalexin. She has no urinary symptoms. Her GP did not prescribe antibiotics at that time as she was asymptomatic. She now presents for review. According to NICE guidance on urinary tract infections in pregnancy, which of the following most accurately describes the management of asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy?',
'Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy does not require treatment as it is a normal finding in 2–10% of pregnant women',
'Treat asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy with a 7-day course of cefalexin 500 mg BD; untreated asymptomatic bacteriuria carries a 20–35% risk of ascending pyelonephritis which can cause preterm labour and low birthweight',
'Treat only if a second MSU confirms the same organism at the same count; single positive MSU has a high false-positive rate',
'Send a repeat MSU and treat only if the patient becomes symptomatic',
'Prescribe nitrofurantoin 50–100 mg QDS for 7 days at all stages of pregnancy for asymptomatic bacteriuria',
'B',
'CORRECT. Asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) in pregnancy is defined as significant bacteriuria (>100,000 CFU/mL) without urinary symptoms. NICE guidance on UTI in pregnancy: ASB in pregnancy MUST be treated with antibiotics even if the patient is completely asymptomatic. Reason: in non-pregnant women, ASB rarely progresses to upper UTI. However, in pregnancy, due to physiological changes (ureteric dilation from progesterone, increased urinary stasis, mechanical compression by the uterus), ASB carries a 20–35% risk of progressing to pyelonephritis — which is associated with preterm labour, low birthweight, and maternal sepsis. Antibiotic choice in pregnancy: cefalexin (7 days), amoxicillin (if sensitive, 7 days), or nitrofurantoin (avoid in first trimester due to potential risk of neural tube defects, and avoid at term due to risk of neonatal haemolytic anaemia). This patient is at 16 weeks — cefalexin or amoxicillin (if sensitive) is appropriate. NICE',
'Incorrect. Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy is NOT a normal finding to be ignored — it requires mandatory treatment. This is one of the most important distinctions between ASB management in pregnant versus non-pregnant women. The risk of progression to pyelonephritis in untreated ASB in pregnancy (20–35%) is far higher than in the non-pregnant population.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. NICE guidance on ASB in pregnancy does not require confirmation with a second MSU before treating. A single MSU with >100,000 CFU/mL of a single organism in a symptomatic or asymptomatic pregnant woman should trigger antibiotic treatment. Waiting for a second MSU introduces an unacceptable delay in treating a condition with significant maternal and foetal risks.',
'Incorrect. Treating only if symptomatic is the appropriate approach for ASB in non-pregnant women — NOT in pregnant women. The physiological changes of pregnancy mean that ASB in pregnancy should always be treated regardless of symptoms, due to the high risk of ascending infection.',
'Incorrect. Nitrofurantoin is contraindicated in the first trimester (potential risk of neural tube defects — avoid <13 weeks) and at term (≥36 weeks — risk of neonatal haemolytic anaemia from immature red blood cell glutathione instability). This patient is at 16 weeks — nitrofurantoin could technically be prescribed at this gestation, but cefalexin or amoxicillin are preferred first-line choices in pregnancy per NICE. Additionally, nitrofurantoin dosing for UTI is 100 mg modified-release BD for 5 days, not 50–100 mg QDS for 7 days (that is the old immediate-release dosing).',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q17 COVID-19 ANTIVIRALS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, chronic kidney disease (eGFR 35 mL/min/1.73m²), and ischaemic heart disease tests positive for COVID-19 on a lateral flow test. He is symptomatic with cough, fever, and fatigue starting 3 days ago. He has not been admitted to hospital. He is vaccinated (4 doses). His SpO2 is 96% on air. His current medications include amlodipine, atorvastatin, ramipril, and aspirin. According to NICE guidance on COVID-19 antivirals and the NHS therapeutic framework, which of the following most accurately describes the treatment options for this patient?',
'This patient does not qualify for antiviral treatment as he is vaccinated and has mild disease',
'Contact the NHS COVID treatment service or refer to the specialist antiviral service — this patient has multiple high-risk conditions (diabetes, CKD stage 3b, ischaemic heart disease) and qualifies for COVID-19 antiviral or neutralising monoclonal antibody treatment within 5 days of symptom onset; nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid) has significant drug interactions that must be checked',
'Prescribe nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid) directly from primary care without checking for drug interactions',
'Admit the patient to hospital for IV remdesivir as he has multiple risk factors',
'Prescribe molnupiravir 800 mg BD for 5 days without specialist referral as it has fewer interactions than Paxlovid',
'B',
'CORRECT. NICE guidance and the NHS therapeutic framework for COVID-19 antivirals identifies high-risk groups eligible for COVID-19 treatments. This patient qualifies on multiple criteria: age ≥65, type 2 diabetes, CKD (eGFR 35 mL/min — Stage 3b-4), and ischaemic heart disease. Eligible patients should be identified and treatment initiated within 5 days of symptom onset (day 3 here — still within the window). Options: (1) Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid): most effective antiviral, reduces hospitalisation by approximately 89% in unvaccinated high-risk patients; HOWEVER — ritonavir is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor with multiple critical drug interactions (atorvastatin must be stopped during Paxlovid, ramipril may need dose adjustment, many other interactions); also requires eGFR ≥30 mL/min for standard dosing; (2) Sotrovimab (neutralising monoclonal antibody) or remdesivir (IV — hospital); (3) Molnupiravir: less effective than Paxlovid, fewer interactions, used when Paxlovid is contraindicated. Specialist referral/contact with the NHS COVID treatment hub is required for assessment and prescription. NICE',
'Incorrect. Vaccination status does not exclude eligibility for COVID-19 antiviral treatment — the high-risk group criteria are based on comorbidities and age, not vaccination status. This patient has multiple qualifying conditions and should be assessed for antiviral treatment within 5 days of symptom onset.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Prescribing nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid) from primary care without checking drug interactions is dangerous. Ritonavir inhibits CYP3A4 — it causes significantly increased plasma levels of many commonly prescribed drugs including statins (risk of myopathy/rhabdomyolysis), immunosuppressants (ciclosporin, tacrolimus), and many others. Atorvastatin must be withheld during Paxlovid treatment. Drug interaction checking is mandatory before prescribing.',
'Incorrect. IV remdesivir is used for hospitalised patients requiring oxygen supplementation — it is not indicated for outpatient mild-moderate COVID-19. This patient has SpO2 96% and does not require hospital admission or IV remdesivir at this stage. Oral antivirals (Paxlovid, molnupiravir) are appropriate for outpatient high-risk patients.',
'Incorrect. Molnupiravir is a valid option when Paxlovid is contraindicated due to drug interactions or eGFR <30 mL/min. However, prescribing it without specialist assessment is not the recommended pathway per the NHS therapeutic framework. The correct process is referral to the specialist COVID antiviral service or NHS treatment hub, not empirical GP prescribing of antivirals without assessment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q18 CELLULITIS MANAGEMENT
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 58-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 3-day history of redness, warmth, swelling, and tenderness of her right lower leg. She has no history of injury. She has type 2 diabetes, obesity (BMI 38 kg/m²), and lymphoedema following right inguinal lymph node dissection for melanoma 3 years ago. On examination, there is a well-demarcated area of erythema, warmth, and oedema extending from the ankle to mid-calf. Her temperature is 37.9°C and heart rate is 94 bpm. There is no blistering, no necrosis, and no crepitus. She is systemically otherwise well. Her WBC is 14.2 × 10⁹/L and CRP is 68 mg/L. According to NICE guidance on cellulitis, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical fusidic acid cream and compression bandaging and review in 5 days',
'Admit to hospital immediately as all cellulitis in diabetic patients requires inpatient IV antibiotics',
'Prescribe oral flucloxacillin 500 mg QDS for 7 days (first-line for non-purulent cellulitis in non-penicillin-allergic patients) and review in 2–3 days; mark the erythema border with a skin marker pen to monitor spread; advise elevation of the limb; review the lymphoedema management',
'Prescribe co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 7 days as broad-spectrum cover is required for diabetic cellulitis',
'Arrange an urgent ultrasound to exclude necrotising fasciitis before starting antibiotics',
'C',
'CORRECT. This patient has non-purulent lower limb cellulitis — most commonly caused by beta-haemolytic Streptococcus (Group A, B, C, or G) or Staphylococcus aureus. Risk factors: diabetes, lymphoedema (major predisposing factor — impaired lymphatic drainage and recurrent bacterial seeding), obesity, and venous insufficiency. NICE guidance on cellulitis: (1) First-line oral antibiotic: flucloxacillin 500 mg QDS for 5–7 days (non-penicillin-allergic, non-purulent cellulitis). If penicillin-allergic: clarithromycin or doxycycline. (2) Mark the erythema border with a skin marker — essential for monitoring response: if erythema spreads beyond the marked border within 24–48 hours, this indicates treatment failure and escalation (IV antibiotics or hospital admission) is needed; (3) Elevate the affected limb to reduce oedema; (4) Review in 2–3 days; (5) This patient does not require hospital admission — she is systemically well with mild tachycardia and low-grade fever but no signs of sepsis, blistering, or necrosis. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical fusidic acid cream has no role in treating cellulitis — cellulitis is a deep dermal and subcutaneous tissue infection, inaccessible to topical antibiotics. Systemic oral antibiotics are required. Compression bandaging over active cellulitis is contraindicated (increases pain and can spread infection).',
'Incorrect. Not all cellulitis in diabetic patients requires hospital admission. NICE guidance recommends admission criteria for cellulitis: systemic sepsis features (hypotension, tachycardia >120, confusion), rapid progression, facial cellulitis, immunocompromise, failure of oral antibiotics after 48 hours, or inability to tolerate oral medication. This patient has mild systemic features and can be managed with oral antibiotics and close review.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Co-amoxiclav has broader spectrum coverage than flucloxacillin but is not the first-line recommendation for non-purulent cellulitis per NICE. It would be appropriate for bite wounds, orbital cellulitis, or cellulitis associated with open wounds. For standard lower limb cellulitis, flucloxacillin is preferred to minimise antimicrobial resistance.',
'Incorrect. Urgent ultrasound to exclude necrotising fasciitis is not required for this presentation. Necrotising fasciitis (NF) is characterised by: rapidly spreading infection with severe disproportionate pain, skin discolouration (grey/dusky), crepitus (gas in tissues), blistering/bullae, systemic sepsis with haemodynamic compromise, and skin necrosis. None of these features are present here. If NF were suspected, immediate surgical consultation — not just ultrasound — would be required.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q19 PERTUSSIS (WHOOPING COUGH)
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP receives a call from a health visitor about a 6-week-old infant who has been brought in by parents with a 10-day history of severe paroxysmal coughing episodes followed by an inspiratory whoop. The infant had an episode of cyanosis during coughing yesterday. He has not yet received any vaccinations (first vaccination due at 8 weeks). His mother received Tdap vaccination at 28 weeks of pregnancy. On examination, the infant appears well between coughing episodes. His SpO2 is 96% between episodes. According to NICE guidance on pertussis and public health management, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Reassure the parents as the infant has partial protection from maternal vaccination and symptoms will resolve within 2 weeks',
'Arrange urgent hospital admission — pertussis in a young infant (under 6 months) carries high risk of apnoea, respiratory failure, and death; notify the health protection team; arrange testing (nasopharyngeal swab/PCR or oral fluid serology); close contacts should receive post-exposure prophylaxis with azithromycin',
'Prescribe oral amoxicillin 125 mg TDS for 5 days as first-line antibiotic for pertussis in infants',
'Prescribe clarithromycin 125 mg BD for 7 days and manage at home as the infant is currently well between episodes',
'Defer notification to the health protection team until laboratory confirmation as clinical diagnosis may be incorrect',
'B',
'CORRECT. Pertussis (whooping cough) in infants under 6 months is a life-threatening emergency — this age group accounts for the majority of pertussis deaths in the UK. Key points: (1) Hospital admission: all infants under 6 months (especially under 3 months) with suspected or confirmed pertussis should be admitted for monitoring due to risk of apnoea, bradycardia, pulmonary hypertension, and respiratory failure; (2) Antibiotics: azithromycin is the first-line treatment for pertussis (most effective when given in the catarrhal phase — first 1–2 weeks of cough). Erythromycin or clarithromycin are alternatives. Amoxicillin has no activity against Bordetella pertussis; (3) Testing: nasopharyngeal PCR swab (most sensitive in first 3 weeks) or oral fluid serology; (4) Notification: pertussis is notifiable — notify the UKHSA health protection team immediately (clinical suspicion is sufficient); (5) Contact management: all close contacts (especially household contacts) should receive post-exposure prophylaxis with azithromycin regardless of vaccination status; (6) Maternal Tdap vaccination reduces but does not eliminate infant pertussis risk. NICE/UKHSA',
'Incorrect. Maternal Tdap vaccination reduces infant pertussis risk by transferring maternal antibodies transplacentally, but protection is not complete. This infant has cyanosis during coughing — a sign of severe disease. An infant of 6 weeks with apnoea/cyanosis during coughing requires urgent hospital admission, not reassurance.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Amoxicillin (and all beta-lactam antibiotics) has NO activity against Bordetella pertussis. Bordetella pertussis is intrinsically resistant to beta-lactam antibiotics. Prescribing amoxicillin for pertussis provides no antimicrobial benefit and would give false reassurance while the infant remains at risk.',
'Incorrect. While clarithromycin is an alternative to azithromycin for pertussis, managing a 6-week-old infant with pertussis and cyanotic episodes at home is not appropriate. Infants under 6 months require hospital admission for monitoring. Managing at home risks missing apnoea episodes that could be fatal without immediate medical intervention.',
'Incorrect. Pertussis is a notifiable disease and must be reported on clinical suspicion — laboratory confirmation is NOT required before notification. Delaying notification to await laboratory results introduces an unacceptable delay in the public health response, which includes contact tracing, post-exposure prophylaxis for close contacts, and school/nursery exclusion guidance.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q20 SCABIES MANAGEMENT
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 45-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 6-week history of intense itching, particularly at night, affecting her wrists, finger web spaces, and abdomen. On examination, there are linear burrows visible between the fingers and on the wrists, with excoriated papules. Her 8-year-old son also has itching affecting his palms and soles. Her husband is asymptomatic. The woman works as a care assistant in a residential care home. According to NICE guidance on scabies management, which of the following most accurately describes the most appropriate treatment strategy?',
'Treat only the symptomatic family members; apply permethrin 5% cream to the affected areas only and wash off after 8 hours',
'Treat all household contacts simultaneously regardless of symptoms; apply permethrin 5% cream to the whole body (from neck to toes in adults; include face and scalp in children and elderly) and leave on for 8–12 hours; repeat after 7 days; notify the infection control team at her care home for workplace management',
'Treat only the woman as the primary case; the son''s symptoms will resolve once she is treated',
'Prescribe oral ivermectin for the woman only as topical treatments are ineffective for chronic scabies',
'Apply permethrin to affected areas only; treat household contacts only if they become symptomatic within 2 weeks',
'B',
'CORRECT. Scabies is caused by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei and is transmitted by prolonged direct skin contact. Critical management principles: (1) Treat ALL household contacts simultaneously — even asymptomatic contacts must be treated on the same day, because: asymptomatic individuals can transmit scabies before symptoms develop (the itching is an allergic reaction to mite products, which takes 4–6 weeks to develop at first infestation); treating only symptomatic individuals results in reinfection; (2) Permethrin 5% cream: apply to the whole body from neck to toes (include face, scalp, and ears in children under 2 and the elderly); leave on for 8–12 hours (overnight) then wash off; repeat after 7 days (to kill mites that hatch from eggs); (3) Bedding and clothing: wash at 60°C or bag for 72 hours on day of treatment; (4) Occupational exposure: the care home must be notified — a scabies outbreak in a care home requires management of all residents and staff simultaneously; (5) Oral ivermectin is used for crusted (Norwegian) scabies or when topical treatment fails. NICE',
'Incorrect. Three errors: (1) only treating symptomatic members will fail — the asymptomatic husband must also be treated simultaneously; (2) permethrin must be applied to the whole body, not just affected areas — mites can be present on any body surface; (3) without care home notification, a large outbreak may continue.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Treating only the woman as the primary case will inevitably result in reinfection from untreated household contacts. All household contacts must be treated simultaneously on the same day. The concept of a "primary case" in scabies management is less important than simultaneous household treatment.',
'Incorrect. Oral ivermectin is not first-line for standard scabies — it is used for crusted (Norwegian) scabies (heavily infested immunocompromised patients), or when topical permethrin has failed on two occasions. Permethrin 5% cream is first-line for typical scabies in the UK.',
'Incorrect. Treating only affected areas misses mites on other body surfaces. Permethrin must be applied to the entire body surface from neck to toes. Treating contacts only if they become symptomatic within 2 weeks is incorrect — all household contacts must be treated simultaneously on the first day of treatment regardless of symptoms.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q21 RABIES POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 32-year-old man presents to his GP 48 hours after returning from a trekking holiday in rural India. While trekking, he was bitten on the hand by a stray dog that appeared unwell. He washed the wound immediately with soap and water for 15 minutes. The bite broke the skin. He has never received rabies vaccination. According to NICE/PHE guidance on rabies post-exposure prophylaxis, which of the following most accurately describes the most appropriate management?',
'Reassure the patient as he washed the wound promptly; rabies prophylaxis is only needed for bat exposures in the UK',
'Prescribe a course of oral amoxicillin for the bite wound infection risk only; rabies risk in India is considered low',
'This is a Category III rabies exposure (bite from a potentially rabid animal in an endemic country) — contact the local health protection team immediately; arrange urgent post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP): human rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) infiltrated around the wound plus the first dose of rabies vaccine; PEP should begin without delay',
'The 48-hour delay since the bite means PEP is no longer effective; reassure the patient and monitor for symptoms',
'Prescribe prophylactic course of co-amoxiclav for bite wound and arrange routine travel health clinic appointment for rabies vaccine course',
'C',
'CORRECT. India is a high-risk country for rabies (one of the highest rabies death rates globally). A bite from a stray dog that appeared unwell in a rabies-endemic country constitutes a Category III rabies exposure (bites or scratches penetrating the skin from a potentially rabid animal). This is a medical emergency requiring immediate PEP. Post-exposure prophylaxis: (1) Human rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG) — infiltrated as much as possible around the wound site to provide immediate passive immunity; (2) Rabies vaccine — first dose on day 0, then days 3, 7, and 14 (if no previous vaccination — 4 doses total); (3) Wound management — thorough washing with soap and water for 15 minutes (already done) plus antiseptic; (4) Timing: PEP can be effective even days to weeks after exposure — the 48-hour delay does NOT make PEP futile. Once clinical rabies develops, it is 100% fatal — PEP must not be delayed for any reason. Contact local health protection team for HRIG supply coordination. NICE/PHE/WHO',
'Incorrect. India is one of the highest-risk countries for rabies globally — it accounts for approximately 35% of global rabies deaths. Reassurance is inappropriate. Prompt wound washing reduces risk but does not eliminate it. Rabies PEP is not exclusively for bat exposures in the UK — any bite from a potentially rabid animal (dog, cat, fox, bat) in an endemic country requires PEP assessment.',
'Incorrect. Amoxicillin for bite wound infection is relevant but the priority is rabies PEP — this is a life-threatening infectious disease risk. Dismissing rabies risk in India as "low" is factually incorrect. Routine travel clinic appointment is entirely inadequate — PEP must begin immediately.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. A 48-hour delay does NOT render PEP ineffective. Rabies has a variable incubation period (typically 1–3 months, but can be as short as 9 days to as long as several years). PEP can be effective even weeks after exposure. There is no time limit beyond which PEP is absolutely futile — it should be started as soon as possible, but the 48-hour delay does not eliminate its benefit.',
'Incorrect. Co-amoxiclav for bite wound prophylaxis is appropriate (dog bites carry Pasteurella, Capnocytophaga, and other organisms) but is entirely inadequate as the sole management for a Category III rabies exposure in India. The rabies risk vastly outweighs the bacterial infection risk in terms of mortality. Arranging a "routine" travel clinic appointment introduces life-threatening delay.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q22 ANTIMICROBIAL RESISTANCE — MRSA
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 68-year-old man who has recently been discharged from hospital after hip replacement surgery attends his GP. A wound swab taken at his pre-operative assessment had grown MRSA (meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) and he was treated with nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine body wash. He now presents with his surgical wound appearing inflamed with a small amount of purulent discharge 10 days post-operatively. A wound swab is taken and grows MRSA (sensitive to vancomycin, teicoplanin, linezolid, trimethoprim, and tetracycline; resistant to flucloxacillin, erythromycin, clindamycin). According to NICE guidance on MRSA management in primary care, which of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic choice for this MRSA wound infection?',
'Prescribe flucloxacillin 500 mg QDS for 7 days as this is first-line for Staphylococcal wound infections',
'Prescribe oral trimethoprim 200 mg BD for 7 days — it is active against this MRSA isolate based on the sensitivity results',
'Prescribe oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days — it is active against this MRSA isolate and is an appropriate oral choice for community-managed MRSA wound infection',
'Prescribe IV vancomycin in primary care as MRSA always requires intravenous glycopeptide antibiotics',
'Prescribe erythromycin 500 mg QDS for 7 days as macrolides cover MRSA adequately',
'C',
'CORRECT. MRSA wound infection requires antibiotics chosen based on the sensitivity pattern — NOT the standard empirical agents (flucloxacillin, which MRSA is inherently resistant to). From the sensitivities provided: active agents include vancomycin (IV), teicoplanin (IV), linezolid (IV/oral — specialist), trimethoprim (oral), and tetracycline/doxycycline (oral). For a mild-moderate community MRSA wound infection not requiring hospital admission: (1) Oral options: doxycycline 100 mg BD, or trimethoprim 200 mg BD, or co-trimoxazole — these are NICE-recommended oral agents for mild MRSA infections; (2) Duration: typically 7–14 days depending on severity; (3) Doxycycline is a reasonable choice — good tissue penetration, oral bioavailability, and active against this isolate. Linezolid (oral) is highly active but reserved for specialist use due to cost and toxicity (myelosuppression, serotonin syndrome risk). Vancomycin is IV only. NICE/PHE MRSA guidelines',
'Incorrect. Flucloxacillin is the drug of choice for meticillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA) infections. MRSA (meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) is inherently resistant to all beta-lactam antibiotics including flucloxacillin, amoxicillin, co-amoxiclav, and cephalosporins. Prescribing flucloxacillin for MRSA would provide no antibacterial activity.',
'Incorrect. Trimethoprim alone is listed as active against this isolate and is a valid option for mild MRSA infections. However, doxycycline has better tissue penetration for skin and soft tissue infections and has established evidence for MRSA skin infections. Trimethoprim is also commonly used for UTIs which may confuse its role. Either could be appropriate — this option is not wrong per se, but doxycycline is a stronger choice for a wound infection.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Vancomycin requires intravenous administration with therapeutic drug monitoring — it is not available for primary care prescribing. It is reserved for severe MRSA infections in hospital (bacteraemia, deep tissue infections, MRSA pneumonia). A mild wound infection can be managed with oral antibiotics in primary care.',
'Incorrect. This MRSA isolate is listed as resistant to erythromycin. Prescribing an antibiotic to which the causative organism is resistant would provide no antimicrobial benefit and would fail to treat the infection. Antibiotic prescribing must always be guided by sensitivity results when a culture is available.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q23 EBV INFECTIOUS MONONUCLEOSIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 19-year-old university student presents to his GP with a 1-week history of severe sore throat, marked fatigue, and cervical lymphadenopathy. His tonsils are grossly enlarged, nearly touching in the midline, with white exudate. His temperature is 38.4°C. On examination, he has posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and mild splenomegaly on abdominal palpation. His blood tests show: WBC 14.8 × 10⁹/L with 55% lymphocytes, 30% of which are atypical lymphocytes on the blood film. His ALT is 78 U/L (mildly elevated). His Monospot (heterophile antibody) test is positive. According to your knowledge of infectious mononucleosis management, which combination of the following pieces of advice is most important to give at this appointment?',
'Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg TDS for 10 days and advise he can return to sport within 1 week',
'Prescribe a course of oral prednisolone to reduce tonsillar swelling and allow immediate return to contact sport',
'Do not prescribe amoxicillin or ampicillin as they cause a florid maculopapular rash in EBV infection; advise strict avoidance of contact sports and heavy lifting for a minimum of 4 weeks due to splenomegaly and risk of splenic rupture; prescribe adequate analgesia',
'Prescribe aciclovir as antiviral treatment significantly reduces the duration and severity of EBV infectious mononucleosis',
'Refer urgently to haematology as atypical lymphocytes on blood film indicate haematological malignancy requiring bone marrow biopsy',
'C',
'CORRECT. Infectious mononucleosis (glandular fever) from Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) — the key management points for the GP are: (1) Avoid amoxicillin and ampicillin — these cause a widespread maculopapular rash in approximately 90–100% of patients with EBV infection (immune-mediated reaction, not true penicillin allergy); penicillin V is safe if a bacterial co-infection needs treating; (2) Splenic rupture risk — splenomegaly is present in approximately 50% of EBV cases and the spleen is friable and vulnerable to rupture with minor trauma. Splenic rupture is the most common fatal complication of EBV. Strict avoidance of contact sports, heavy lifting, and strenuous physical activity for at least 4 weeks; (3) Management: supportive — analgesia (paracetamol/ibuprofen), fluids, rest; (4) Aciclovir (option D): does NOT improve clinical outcomes in EBV infectious mononucleosis — it has activity against EBV in vitro but RCTs show no clinical benefit; (5) Atypical lymphocytes (option E): reactive change typical of EBV — not malignancy. NICE',
'Incorrect. Amoxicillin is absolutely contraindicated in EBV infectious mononucleosis — prescribing it would cause a florid maculopapular rash in approximately 90–100% of EBV-infected patients. Advising a return to sport within 1 week in a patient with splenomegaly risks fatal splenic rupture.',
'Incorrect. Oral prednisolone may be considered for severe tonsillar swelling causing airway compromise — but it is not routinely recommended for EBV and should not be used as a reason to allow early return to contact sport. Steroids do not reduce splenic rupture risk. The advice to allow immediate return to contact sport in a patient with splenomegaly is potentially fatal.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Aciclovir has no proven clinical benefit in EBV infectious mononucleosis. It is active against EBV in vitro but does not alter the clinical course (severity, duration) of the illness. It is not recommended by NICE for EBV.',
'Incorrect. Atypical lymphocytes (reactive lymphocytes) on blood film in the context of EBV infectious mononucleosis are a reactive immune response to viral infection — they represent activated cytotoxic T-cells responding to EBV-infected B-lymphocytes. They are NOT malignant and do not require haematology referral or bone marrow biopsy. The clinical context (positive Monospot, mononucleosis syndrome) clearly explains these findings.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q24 CRYPTOSPORIDIOSIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 34-year-old man presents to his GP with a 10-day history of profuse watery diarrhoea (8–12 times per day), abdominal cramps, and nausea. He has no blood in his stool and no fever. He visited a petting farm with his children 2 weeks ago. He is otherwise fit and well. An HIV test is negative. His stool microscopy and culture shows: no bacterial pathogens isolated; Cryptosporidium oocysts identified on Ziehl-Neelsen staining. He is becoming increasingly dehydrated. According to NICE guidance on gastroenteritis and public health obligations, which of the following most accurately describes the management of cryptosporidiosis in an immunocompetent patient?',
'Prescribe oral metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 7 days as this is the treatment for cryptosporidiosis',
'Prescribe oral nitazoxanide 500 mg BD for 3 days as this is the specific treatment for cryptosporidiosis in immunocompetent adults',
'Cryptosporidiosis in immunocompetent individuals is largely self-limiting; management is supportive (oral rehydration, anti-diarrhoeal agents if tolerated); notify the local health protection team as cryptosporidiosis is a notifiable disease; the patient should not swim in public swimming pools until 2 weeks after diarrhoea has resolved',
'Prescribe oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD for 5 days as fluoroquinolones are active against Cryptosporidium',
'Prescribe oral azithromycin 500 mg OD for 3 days as macrolides are effective against intestinal protozoa',
'C',
'CORRECT. Cryptosporidiosis (caused by Cryptosporidium parvum or C. hominis — an intestinal protozoan parasite) in immunocompetent individuals is typically self-limiting, resolving within 2–3 weeks. Management: (1) Supportive care: oral rehydration solution (ORS) to prevent dehydration — key management; loperamide can be used with caution for symptomatic relief in non-bloody diarrhoea; (2) No specific antiparasitic treatment is routinely required in immunocompetent patients — nitazoxanide has limited evidence in immunocompetent adults and is not widely available in the UK; (3) Metronidazole, ciprofloxacin, and azithromycin have no activity against Cryptosporidium; (4) Notification: cryptosporidiosis is a notifiable disease (Category A) — notify the UKHSA health protection team; (5) Exclusion from swimming pools: crucial — Cryptosporidium is highly chlorine-resistant and swimming while infected causes pool contamination and outbreaks; exclude until 2 weeks after diarrhoea resolves. In immunocompromised patients (HIV CD4 <50, organ transplant recipients), cryptosporidiosis is life-threatening and requires specialist management. NICE',
'Incorrect. Metronidazole has no activity against Cryptosporidium species — Cryptosporidium is a coccidian parasite (not an amoeba or anaerobic bacterium). Prescribing metronidazole for cryptosporidiosis provides no antimicrobial benefit. Metronidazole is effective against Giardia, amoebae, and anaerobic bacteria.',
'Incorrect. Nitazoxanide does have some evidence for reducing the duration of cryptosporidiosis in immunocompetent patients (but limited and not robust). It is not routinely prescribed in the UK for immunocompetent adults as cryptosporidiosis is self-limiting. In severe cases or immunocompromised patients, specialist input may lead to its use, but it is not the standard management for a fit adult with self-limiting cryptosporidiosis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Ciprofloxacin (a fluoroquinolone) targets bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV — it has no activity against Cryptosporidium, which is a protozoan parasite not susceptible to antibacterial antibiotics.',
'Incorrect. Azithromycin (a macrolide) targets bacterial ribosomal protein synthesis. It has no proven efficacy against Cryptosporidium in immunocompetent patients. Some macrolides have been investigated for cryptosporidiosis but are not standard treatment in the UK.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q25 TRAVEL-ASSOCIATED DIARRHOEA
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 30-year-old woman presents to her GP 3 days after returning from a 2-week holiday in Thailand. She has had 5–7 loose watery stools per day for the past 4 days, with mild abdominal cramping. She has no blood in her stool, no fever, and is tolerating oral fluids. She feels generally unwell but is able to walk in. On examination, she is mildly dehydrated. Her abdomen is soft with mild generalised tenderness. Stool culture is pending. According to NICE guidance on travellers'' diarrhoea management, which of the following is the most appropriate management at this stage?',
'Prescribe oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD for 3 days as fluoroquinolone antibiotics are first-line for all travellers'' diarrhoea from Asia',
'Advise oral rehydration with ORS sachets or fluid replacement; prescribe loperamide 4 mg initially then 2 mg after each loose stool for symptomatic relief (max 16 mg/24 hours) for mild-moderate travellers'' diarrhoea without red flag features; safety-net clearly',
'Prescribe oral metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 5 days as Giardia is the most common cause of travellers'' diarrhoea from Thailand',
'Admit to hospital for IV rehydration as she has been unwell for 4 days',
'Prescribe co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 5 days as broad-spectrum cover is required for tropical diarrhoeal illness',
'B',
'CORRECT. Travellers'' diarrhoea (TD) is the most common travel-related illness, affecting up to 40% of travellers to high-risk regions (South Asia, Southeast Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, Latin America). Most cases are bacterial (ETEC — enterotoxigenic E. coli, most common; also Campylobacter, Shigella, Salmonella). Management: (1) Mild-moderate TD without red flags (non-bloody stool, no fever, tolerated orally, not dehydrated): supportive — ORS for rehydration, loperamide for symptomatic relief (reduces stool frequency, safe in non-bloody diarrhoea); (2) Antibiotics: NICE and PHE guidance supports reserving antibiotics for moderate-severe TD (>3 stools/day despite loperamide, significant fever, bloody diarrhoea, systemic illness). Ciprofloxacin resistance is high in Thailand and Southeast Asia (>50% Campylobacter resistance) — azithromycin is preferred for empirical antibiotic treatment of TD from Asia; (3) Red flags warranting immediate review: blood in stool, high fever, inability to maintain hydration, immunocompromise. NICE/PHE travellers'' diarrhoea guidance',
'Incorrect. Ciprofloxacin is NOT the preferred empirical antibiotic for travellers'' diarrhoea from Thailand and Southeast Asia — fluoroquinolone resistance (particularly in Campylobacter) exceeds 50% in this region. If antibiotics are indicated, azithromycin 500 mg OD for 3 days is the preferred empirical treatment for TD from Asia. Moreover, this patient has mild-moderate TD and does not yet meet the threshold for antibiotic treatment — supportive care is first-line.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Giardia causes a subacute/chronic diarrhoeal illness (fatty stools, bloating, flatulence, nausea) typically developing 1–3 weeks after exposure and lasting weeks to months. It is less likely to cause the acute watery diarrhoea of 4 days duration described here. While metronidazole treats Giardia, empirical metronidazole for all acute travellers'' diarrhoea is not appropriate without evidence of Giardia or anaerobic infection.',
'Incorrect. Hospital admission for IV rehydration is appropriate for: severe dehydration (inability to maintain oral hydration, signs of circulatory compromise, confusion), bloody diarrhoea, severe systemic illness, or immunocompromise. This patient is mildly dehydrated, tolerating fluids, and not acutely unwell — she does not meet criteria for hospital admission.',
'Incorrect. Co-amoxiclav is not recommended for travellers'' diarrhoea — it is predominantly active against gram-positive organisms and has limited activity against the gram-negative enteropathogens (ETEC, Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter) that cause most cases of TD. It also carries a higher risk of C. difficile colitis.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q26 TYPHOID FEVER
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 28-year-old woman presents to her GP after returning from a 3-week visit to rural Pakistan 10 days ago. She has had a 6-day history of worsening fever (currently 40°C), frontal headache, malaise, and relative bradycardia (pulse 68 bpm despite high fever). She has mild abdominal discomfort. On examination, she has mild splenomegaly and a few faint rose-coloured spots (rose spots) on her abdomen. Her blood tests show: WBC 3.2 × 10⁹/L (leucopenia), CRP 42 mg/L, ALT 64 U/L, and platelets 128 × 10⁹/L (mild thrombocytopenia). Which of the following most accurately identifies the likely diagnosis and the most appropriate investigation to confirm it?',
'Malaria; arrange urgent malaria thick and thin films and rapid antigen test immediately',
'Typhoid fever (enteric fever); the most sensitive investigation is blood culture (Salmonella typhi — positive in 70–90% of cases in the first 2 weeks); arrange urgent blood cultures and notify the health protection team',
'Dengue fever; arrange dengue NS1 antigen and serology; no specific treatment available',
'Infectious mononucleosis (EBV); arrange Monospot test and EBV serology',
'Brucellosis; arrange brucella serology and blood culture for Brucella species',
'B',
'CORRECT. The clinical presentation is classic for typhoid fever (Salmonella typhi infection — enteric fever): (1) History of travel to South Asia (Pakistan — high-risk typhoid area); (2) Step-ladder pyrexia — progressive fever increasing daily; (3) Relative bradycardia (pulse-temperature dissociation — paradoxically slow pulse for the degree of fever); (4) Rose spots — faint, salmon-coloured, blanching maculopapular lesions on the abdomen in approximately 30% of cases; (5) Splenomegaly; (6) Leucopenia (characteristic — unlike most bacterial infections which cause leucocytosis); (7) Mild thrombocytopenia and transaminitis. Investigation of choice: blood culture (Salmonella typhi) — most sensitive in the first 2 weeks (70–90% positive). Additional investigations: bone marrow culture (most sensitive — >90% even after antibiotics started), stool and urine cultures (later in illness). Typhoid is a notifiable disease. Complications: intestinal perforation (most feared), haemorrhage, encephalopathy. NICE/PHE',
'Incorrect. Malaria must always be excluded in a febrile traveller from an endemic area — it is correct to arrange urgent malaria films. However, the combination of relative bradycardia, rose spots, splenomegaly, leucopenia, and step-ladder fever is more specific to typhoid. Importantly, in a febrile traveller from malaria-endemic areas, BOTH malaria tests and blood cultures for typhoid should be arranged — they are not mutually exclusive investigations.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Dengue fever presents with: sudden high fever, severe headache, retro-orbital pain, severe myalgia ("breakbone fever"), a distinctive maculopapular rash, and characteristic thrombocytopenia with leucopenia. While some features overlap with typhoid, the relative bradycardia, rose spots, and progressive step-ladder fever are not features of dengue. Dengue is self-limiting in most cases, but severe dengue (haemorrhagic/shock syndrome) requires hospital management.',
'Incorrect. EBV infectious mononucleosis would be unusual in a 28-year-old returning from travel without a sore throat or significant cervical lymphadenopathy. EBV does not cause rose spots or relative bradycardia. The travel history and specific clinical features (rose spots, relative bradycardia, leucopenia) strongly suggest typhoid over EBV.',
'Incorrect. Brucellosis occurs from contact with infected animals or consumption of unpasteurised dairy products — it is common in rural Pakistan and should be considered. However, rose spots and relative bradycardia are not features of brucellosis. Brucellosis presents with undulant fever, profuse sweating, arthralgia, and hepatosplenomegaly. Blood culture for Brucella is appropriate in the differential but is less likely than typhoid given the clinical picture.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q27 HEPATITIS B VACCINATION
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 35-year-old man who is a healthcare worker presents to his occupational health GP for a pre-employment health check. He reports receiving the full 3-dose hepatitis B vaccination schedule (0, 1, and 6 months) 3 years ago when he started a previous job. His anti-HBs level is 8 mIU/mL (checked today). According to NICE and PHE guidance on hepatitis B vaccination and occupational immunisation, which of the following most accurately describes his vaccination status and the most appropriate action?',
'He is adequately protected; anti-HBs of 8 mIU/mL is above the protective threshold of 5 mIU/mL',
'He is a non-responder; give one booster dose and recheck anti-HBs 4–8 weeks later; if still <10 mIU/mL after the booster, he is a true non-responder and should be tested for occult hepatitis B infection',
'He requires a repeat full 3-dose course of hepatitis B vaccination as his immunity has waned',
'He is adequately protected for life; anti-HBs levels naturally decline and do not need monitoring in healthcare workers',
'Give 3 further booster doses at monthly intervals as accelerated re-immunisation is required for all healthcare workers with anti-HBs <100 mIU/mL',
'B',
'CORRECT. PHE guidance on hepatitis B vaccination for healthcare workers defines: Anti-HBs ≥100 mIU/mL at 4–8 weeks post-vaccination: adequate responder — likely to be protected long-term even as levels decline. Anti-HBs 10–99 mIU/mL: adequate response but lower level — may need boosting earlier. Anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL: inadequate response — may be a non-responder (5–10% of vaccines) or a poor responder. Management for anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL: (1) Administer one further dose of hepatitis B vaccine; (2) Recheck anti-HBs 4–8 weeks after the booster dose; (3) If anti-HBs rises to ≥10 mIU/mL: adequate response — complete a second full course (total 6 doses). (4) If anti-HBs remains <10 mIU/mL after additional doses: test for HBsAg and anti-HBc (to exclude occult/chronic HBV infection as a cause of non-response). True non-responders may require hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG) post-exposure. PHE/NICE occupational vaccination guidance',
'Incorrect. Anti-HBs of 8 mIU/mL is BELOW the protective threshold of 10 mIU/mL (not 5 mIU/mL — the threshold is 10 mIU/mL). This level indicates inadequate vaccination response and a booster dose is required. Anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL does not provide reliable protection against hepatitis B infection.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. A full repeat 3-dose course is not the first step for anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL — a single booster dose is given first, and the response assessed. Only if the patient fails to respond to the booster is a full second course considered. Starting immediately with a repeat 3-dose course before assessing the booster response is not the recommended protocol.',
'Incorrect. Anti-HBs levels DO need monitoring in healthcare workers — they can wane over time and may fall below the protective threshold. Healthcare workers should have anti-HBs checked 4–8 weeks after completing the vaccination course and periodically thereafter. Some trusts check levels every 5 years. Claiming lifelong protection without monitoring is incorrect.',
'Incorrect. Three monthly booster doses is not the recommended protocol for anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL. The protocol is: one booster dose → recheck at 4–8 weeks → if still inadequate, give a second full course. Monthly doses are not part of the standard hepatitis B vaccination protocol for non-responders.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q28 SEPTIC ARTHRITIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 72-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and rheumatoid arthritis (on methotrexate 15 mg weekly and hydroxychloroquine) presents to her GP with a 2-day history of severe pain, swelling, and warmth of her right knee. She is unable to fully flex or extend the knee. Her temperature is 38.5°C. Her right knee is hot, tender, and markedly swollen with a large effusion. She has no recent skin break or trauma. Her CRP is 186 mg/L and WBC is 17.2 × 10⁹/L. According to your knowledge of septic arthritis diagnosis and management, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?',
'Prescribe oral co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 7 days and review in 48 hours',
'Prescribe oral prednisolone 40 mg OD as this is likely a rheumatoid arthritis flare requiring systemic steroid treatment',
'Refer immediately for same-day hospital assessment and joint aspiration — septic arthritis is a medical and orthopaedic emergency; treatment must not be delayed as irreversible joint damage occurs within days of infection',
'Arrange urgent MRI of the knee before starting any treatment to distinguish septic arthritis from RA flare',
'Prescribe IV methotrexate to increase the immunosuppressive effect and reduce joint inflammation',
'C',
'CORRECT. Septic arthritis is an orthopaedic emergency. Key features in this patient: acute monoarthropathy (one joint — right knee) with fever, extreme warmth, swelling, and severe restriction of movement in an immunocompromised patient (diabetes + methotrexate + hydroxychloroquine — triple immunosuppression). The extremely elevated CRP (186 mg/L) and WBC (17.2 × 10⁹/L) confirm significant systemic sepsis. Distinguishing from RA flare: RA flares are typically polyarticular (not monoarticular), develop gradually, without fever, and without such dramatically elevated inflammatory markers. Management: (1) Same-day emergency hospital admission — do not delay; (2) Joint aspiration (arthrocentesis): must be performed urgently before antibiotics if possible (for Gram stain, culture, white cell count, crystal analysis); synovial WBC >50,000 cells/µL with >75% neutrophils suggests septic arthritis; (3) IV antibiotics: typically flucloxacillin (for Staph aureus — most common cause) empirically pending cultures; (4) Orthopaedic review for washout if needed. NICE',
'Incorrect. Oral co-amoxiclav for a patient with suspected septic arthritis is wholly inadequate — IV antibiotics are required as intra-articular antibiotic concentrations must be achieved rapidly. A 48-hour review for a condition that can destroy a joint within days is inappropriate. Same-day hospital admission is required.',
'Incorrect. While an RA flare is in the differential, prescribing prednisolone for a hot, swollen joint with fever and dramatically elevated inflammatory markers risks immunosuppressing a patient with active septic arthritis — this would worsen the infection. Septic arthritis must be excluded before giving corticosteroids. Methotrexate should also be stopped during acute septic arthritis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. MRI of the knee is not the first investigation for suspected septic arthritis — joint aspiration and urgent hospital assessment are the immediate priorities. MRI provides excellent soft tissue and bone detail but cannot reliably distinguish infection from inflammatory arthropathy and adds unacceptable delay. Joint aspiration is the diagnostic and therapeutic priority.',
'Incorrect. IV methotrexate is not a treatment for septic arthritis. Methotrexate (used for RA) is an immunosuppressant — increasing it in the context of active joint infection would worsen the infectious process. Methotrexate should be STOPPED during active sepsis and infection per NICE guidance on RA management during intercurrent illness.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q29 CLOSTRIDIUM DIFFICILE — SEVERE EPISODE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 78-year-old man is reviewed at home by his GP. He has been on co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for the past 8 days for a lower respiratory tract infection. He now has a 3-day history of profuse watery diarrhoea (10–12 times daily), severe abdominal pain and distension, and high fever (39.2°C). He is confused and his blood pressure is 88/60 mmHg. His abdomen is distended and tympanic with tenderness throughout. His CRP yesterday was 188 mg/L. A stool sample sent yesterday is positive for C. difficile toxin. According to NICE guidance on C. difficile management (NG199), which of the following most accurately describes the severity classification and immediate management?',
'This is a first episode of non-severe C. difficile; prescribe oral vancomycin 125 mg QDS for 10 days and review in 48 hours',
'This is severe C. difficile infection; call 999 for emergency hospital admission — features of severe disease (fever ≥38.5°C, WBC >15, rising creatinine, abdominal distension) and haemodynamic compromise indicate risk of toxic megacolon requiring IV fluids, IV antibiotics (IV metronidazole or oral vancomycin) and surgical review',
'This is moderate C. difficile; prescribe oral metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 10 days and review in 48 hours',
'Stop the co-amoxiclav only and recheck the stool toxin in 5 days as stopping the precipitating antibiotic is often sufficient for mild-moderate C. difficile',
'Prescribe fidaxomicin 200 mg BD for 10 days in primary care as this is the preferred agent for severe C. difficile',
'B',
'CORRECT. This patient has SEVERE C. difficile infection with haemodynamic compromise — a medical emergency. NICE NG199 severity criteria for C. difficile: Non-severe: diarrhoea only, not systemically unwell. Severe (ANY ONE of): WBC >15 × 10⁹/L, rising creatinine (>50% above baseline), temperature ≥38.5°C, evidence of severe colitis (radiological or clinical — abdominal distension, tenderness). Life-threatening/fulminant: hypotension, partial/complete ileus, toxic megacolon, colonic perforation. This patient has: fever 39.2°C, haemodynamic compromise (BP 88/60), confusion, severe abdominal distension, CRP 188 — this is LIFE-THREATENING C. difficile with risk of toxic megacolon. Management: 999 emergency transfer for: IV fluids, IV antibiotics (vancomycin orally + IV metronidazole for severe/fulminant disease), surgical review (colectomy may be needed for toxic megacolon), intensive monitoring. Stop co-amoxiclav immediately. NICE NG199',
'Incorrect. This is not non-severe C. difficile — oral vancomycin 125 mg QDS and 48-hour review would be dangerously inadequate for a patient in haemodynamic shock with a distended abdomen and severe systemic toxicity. This patient requires emergency hospital admission.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Oral metronidazole is no longer recommended by NICE NG199 as first-line treatment for C. difficile (updated 2021 guidance). Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin are the recommended agents. More importantly, this patient has life-threatening disease requiring emergency admission — oral antibiotics alone without hospital-level care are insufficient.',
'Incorrect. Simply stopping the co-amoxiclav without treating the C. difficile infection is not appropriate for any severity of C. difficile with ongoing symptoms. Stopping the precipitating antibiotic is an important adjunct but not a standalone treatment. This patient has severe life-threatening disease requiring emergency hospital management.',
'Incorrect. Fidaxomicin is the preferred oral agent for C. difficile per NICE NG199 (better recurrence rates than vancomycin). However, for fulminant/life-threatening C. difficile with haemodynamic compromise, the priority is emergency hospital admission — not community prescribing of fidaxomicin. IV antibiotics alongside oral vancomycin are used in hospital for severe/fulminant disease. GP prescribing of fidaxomicin for a patient in septic shock is not the appropriate management.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q30 HAND HYGIENE AND INFECTION CONTROL
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP is reviewing infection control practices in their surgery. A patient with confirmed norovirus gastroenteritis attended the surgery earlier in the day, and several other patients used the waiting room and the consultation room. The receptionist has used alcohol hand gel when cleaning the surfaces and asks if this is adequate. According to your knowledge of norovirus infection control and evidence-based hand hygiene, which of the following most accurately describes the limitation of alcohol hand gel for norovirus and the recommended approach?',
'Alcohol hand gel is equally effective against norovirus as against all other pathogens; no additional measures are required',
'Norovirus is an enveloped virus easily killed by standard 70% isopropyl alcohol gel; hand gel is fully effective',
'Norovirus is a non-enveloped virus that is resistant to alcohol hand gel at standard concentrations; soap and water hand washing is more effective; surfaces should be decontaminated with 1000 ppm chlorine-based disinfectant (bleach solution); clinical areas should be thoroughly cleaned',
'Norovirus does not survive on surfaces for more than 30 minutes; standard surface cleaning with soap and water is sufficient',
'All patients who attended the same waiting room as a norovirus patient must be notified and tested for norovirus',
'C',
'CORRECT. Norovirus (the most common cause of viral gastroenteritis, also known as winter vomiting disease) has important infection control implications: (1) Non-enveloped virus: norovirus lacks a lipid envelope — alcohol-based hand gels (which work by disrupting lipid envelopes) are significantly less effective against non-enveloped viruses including norovirus, hepatitis A, and adenovirus. Hand washing with soap and water (mechanical removal of viral particles) is more effective for norovirus than alcohol gel; (2) Surface survival: norovirus can survive on environmental surfaces for days to weeks; (3) Disinfection: sodium hypochlorite (bleach) at 1000 ppm is the recommended surface disinfectant for norovirus (higher concentration — 10,000 ppm — for contaminated surfaces after vomiting or diarrhoea); (4) Outbreak settings (care homes, hospitals): isolation, cohorting of ill patients, enhanced cleaning; (5) Staff exclusion: symptomatic staff should not return to food handling or clinical work until 48 hours after their last episode of vomiting/diarrhoea. NICE/PHE norovirus guidance',
'Incorrect. Alcohol hand gel is NOT equally effective against all pathogens. It is highly effective against enveloped viruses (influenza, coronaviruses, HIV) and most bacteria, but has significantly reduced efficacy against non-enveloped viruses (norovirus, hepatitis A, adenovirus) and bacterial spores (C. difficile). Additional cleaning measures are required for norovirus.',
'Incorrect. Norovirus is NOT an enveloped virus — it is a non-enveloped RNA virus (Caliciviridae family). Enveloped viruses (influenza, coronaviruses, RSV, herpes viruses, HIV) are readily killed by alcohol gel. Non-enveloped viruses are significantly more resistant to alcohol. This is a critical distinction for infection control practice.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Norovirus can survive on hard surfaces for days to weeks (up to 2 weeks in some studies). A 30-minute window before surfaces become safe is incorrect. This is why norovirus causes prolonged outbreaks in healthcare settings and cruise ships — it persists in the environment and is difficult to decontaminate without appropriate chlorine-based disinfectants.',
'Incorrect. Contact tracing and testing of all waiting room attendees is not required for standard norovirus cases in primary care. Norovirus is extremely common, typically mild, and self-limiting in immunocompetent individuals. Enhanced infection control measures (cleaning, hand hygiene advice for staff) are appropriate, but mass testing is not indicated.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q31 LATENT TB TREATMENT
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 28-year-old Bangladeshi woman has been living in the UK for 3 years. She presents to her GP following a positive QuantiFERON-TB Gold IGRA result (3.8 IU/mL, threshold >0.35 IU/mL) identified during new entrant TB screening. She has no symptoms of active TB — no cough, haemoptysis, weight loss, fever, or night sweats. Her chest X-ray is normal. She is HIV-negative, has no immunosuppression, and is not pregnant. According to NICE guidance on TB (NG33) and latent TB infection treatment, which of the following treatment regimens is the recommended first-line option for latent TB infection in this patient?',
'Rifampicin 10 mg/kg OD for 6 months (6R) as rifampicin monotherapy is the NICE-recommended first-line treatment for latent TB',
'Isoniazid 300 mg OD + rifampicin 600 mg OD for 3 months (3HR) — this is the NICE-recommended first-line treatment for latent TB infection; it has equivalent efficacy to 6 months of isoniazid monotherapy with better adherence and completion rates',
'Isoniazid 300 mg OD monotherapy for 6 months (6H) — this is the only recommended LTBI treatment as combination therapy increases hepatotoxicity risk',
'Full active TB treatment (rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) for 6 months as IGRA positivity indicates active TB infection',
'No treatment is required as LTBI in young adults has a low lifetime risk of reactivation',
'B',
'CORRECT. NICE NG33 (updated guidance on tuberculosis) recommends the following first-line treatments for latent TB infection (LTBI): (1) 3 months of isoniazid + rifampicin (3HR): NICE 2016 update recommended 3HR as first-line LTBI treatment for most patients. It has equivalent efficacy to 6 months of isoniazid (6H), with better adherence and completion rates (shorter course), and equivalent hepatotoxicity profile. This is the preferred option for most patients including this woman. (2) 6 months of isoniazid (6H): alternative option, particularly if rifampicin is contraindicated (drug interactions — rifampicin is a potent CYP450 inducer). (3) Pre-treatment assessment: LFTs baseline (especially important given age, ethnicity — South Asian women have higher hepatotoxicity risk), exclude active TB before starting treatment. Active TB (option D) requires 4-drug RIPE therapy — IGRA positivity with normal CXR and no symptoms indicates LATENT infection, not active disease. NICE NG33',
'Incorrect. Rifampicin monotherapy (6R) is sometimes used for LTBI when isoniazid is contraindicated (isoniazid resistance or intolerance), but it is not NICE first-line — the recommended regimen is combined isoniazid + rifampicin (3HR). Rifampicin monotherapy risks selecting rifampicin-resistant organisms if active disease is inadvertently treated.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Isoniazid monotherapy for 6 months (6H) is a valid alternative LTBI treatment, but NICE NG33 updated guidance recommends 3HR (3 months of isoniazid + rifampicin) as first-line over 6H, primarily due to better adherence rates with the shorter 3-month regimen. 6H is not the only recommended regimen.',
'Incorrect. A positive IGRA with normal chest X-ray and no symptoms represents LATENT TB infection (LTBI) — not active TB disease. Active TB requires: positive IGRA/TST PLUS symptoms OR radiological changes OR bacteriological evidence. LTBI is treated with preventive therapy (3HR or 6H) — the full 4-drug RIPE regimen is used for active disease only.',
'Incorrect. While the lifetime risk of LTBI reactivation is approximately 5–10% in immunocompetent individuals, this patient has several higher-risk features: young age (immune system may not suppress TB indefinitely), recent migration from a high-prevalence country (Bangladesh — likely recent infection within the last 2 years, higher reactivation risk), and positive IGRA at a young age. NICE NG33 specifically recommends LTBI treatment for new entrants from high-incidence countries with a positive IGRA — observation alone is not appropriate.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q32 POST-SPLENECTOMY MANAGEMENT
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 32-year-old man underwent emergency splenectomy 3 months ago following a road traffic accident. He attends his GP surgery for his first review since discharge. His discharge letter mentions a post-splenectomy protocol but he has not yet received any vaccines or prophylactic antibiotics. He is otherwise well. According to NICE guidance and the British Society for Haematology (BSH) guidelines on post-splenectomy management, which of the following most accurately describes the vaccinations and prophylaxis required?',
'No special management is needed as the spleen regenerates over time and full immune function returns within 2 years',
'Administer all required vaccinations regardless of timing since surgery; prescribe lifelong penicillin V prophylaxis; provide the patient with a splenectomy alert card',
'Post-splenectomy patients are only at risk from capsulated bacteria in the first year after surgery; prophylaxis beyond 12 months is not required',
'Administer vaccinations against encapsulated bacteria (pneumococcal, Hib, meningococcal ACWY and B, influenza); prescribe lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis (phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg BD or amoxicillin 500 mg OD, or erythromycin if penicillin-allergic); issue a splenectomy card and educate about overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI)',
'Post-splenectomy management is the responsibility of secondary care; the GP has no role in vaccinations or prophylaxis',
'D',
'CORRECT. Asplenic patients are at lifelong increased risk of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (OPSI) — particularly from encapsulated bacteria (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis). OPSI is a rapidly fatal syndrome with mortality up to 50% in 24 hours. Post-splenectomy protocol per BSH/NICE: (1) Vaccinations: pneumococcal (PPV23 and PCV13 ideally both), Hib (if not previously immunised), meningococcal ACWY conjugate, meningococcal B, and annual influenza. Ideally given ≥2 weeks before elective splenectomy or ≥2 weeks after emergency splenectomy; (2) Lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis: phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin V) 500 mg BD, or amoxicillin 500 mg OD (if penicillin-allergic: erythromycin or doxycycline); (3) Splenectomy patient alert card (carried at all times); (4) Patient education: OPSI warning symptoms (fever, chills, rigors requiring immediate medical attention and empirical antibiotics), travel advice, malaria prophylaxis urgency, animal bite risk (Capnocytophaga from dog/cat bites — severe risk in asplenic patients). This patient has not received any vaccinations or prophylaxis — urgent correction is required. BSH/NICE',
'Incorrect. The spleen does not regenerate after complete splenectomy. Accessory spleens may hypertrophy but do not restore immune function fully. The immune deficiency is permanent — lifelong prophylaxis and monitoring are required.',
'Incorrect. While vaccinations should be given as soon as reasonably possible after surgery (ideally ≥2 weeks post-emergency splenectomy), they should still be administered even if the 2-week window has passed. The 3-month delay here means vaccinations are overdue but still necessary. All vaccinations and antibiotic prophylaxis should be started immediately.',
'Incorrect. Post-splenectomy patients are at lifelong increased risk from encapsulated bacteria — not just in the first year. OPSI risk is highest in the first 2–3 years but persists for life. Stopping prophylaxis after 12 months significantly increases OPSI risk. Lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended by BSH.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. GPs play a central role in post-splenectomy management. Primary care responsibilities include: vaccination administration and scheduling, annual influenza vaccination, prescribing and monitoring lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis, issuing/replacing splenectomy alert cards, and ensuring the patient receives appropriate travel health advice. This is not exclusively a secondary care responsibility.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q33 DENGUE FEVER
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 26-year-old man presents to his GP 7 days after returning from Bali, Indonesia. He has had 5 days of high fever (39.5°C), severe headache, intense retro-orbital pain worsening with eye movement, severe myalgia ("as if my bones are breaking"), and a rash that appeared 3 days into the illness. On examination, he has a macular-papular rash on his trunk and limbs, mild facial flushing, and appears systemically unwell. His blood tests show: WBC 2.8 × 10⁹/L (leucopenia), platelets 68 × 10⁹/L (thrombocytopenia), haematocrit 46%, and ALT 88 U/L. There is no bleeding from any site. According to your knowledge of dengue fever, which of the following most accurately identifies the diagnosis and the most important monitoring parameter indicating progression to severe dengue?',
'Malaria; arrange urgent thick and thin blood films and treat empirically with artemether-lumefantrine pending results',
'Dengue fever; the most important marker of progression to severe dengue/dengue haemorrhagic fever is rapidly rising haematocrit (haemoconcentration >20% from baseline) indicating plasma leakage, combined with falling platelet count; refer to hospital for monitoring',
'Chikungunya fever; the joint pain and rash are characteristic; prescribe NSAIDs and reassure that resolution typically occurs within 2 weeks',
'Typhoid fever; arrange blood cultures and prescribe oral azithromycin pending culture results',
'Zika virus infection; refer to sexual health clinic for partner notification as Zika is sexually transmissible',
'B',
'CORRECT. Dengue fever is caused by dengue virus (DENV 1–4, flavivirus) transmitted by Aedes aegypti mosquitoes, endemic in Southeast Asia, South America, and the Caribbean. Classic triad: high fever + severe headache with retro-orbital pain + severe myalgia/arthralgia ("breakbone fever"). The rash in dengue: appears on days 3–6 of illness, characteristically with "islands of white in a sea of red" (pale areas within the erythema). Key laboratory findings: leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, and elevated transaminases. Progression to severe dengue: the critical phase (days 3–7 of illness) involves potential plasma leakage from capillary permeability — monitored by: (1) Rising haematocrit (>20% rise from baseline = haemoconcentration indicating plasma leakage); (2) Rapidly falling platelet count (<100,000/µL is warning; <20,000/µL is severe); (3) Warning signs: abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, bleeding, rapid breathing, fatigue, postural hypotension. This patient requires hospital monitoring — no specific antiviral treatment exists; management is supportive (IV fluids titrated carefully to avoid fluid overload). Aspirin and NSAIDs are contraindicated (platelet dysfunction + anticoagulation risk). NICE/PHE',
'Incorrect. While malaria must always be excluded in a febrile traveller from Southeast Asia and blood films should be performed, the clinical picture is more consistent with dengue: the specific retro-orbital pain, severe myalgia ("breakbone fever"), and the rash appearing on day 3 are characteristic of dengue. However, malaria testing should be performed concurrently — the two are not mutually exclusive.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Chikungunya fever causes severe, debilitating arthralgia/arthritis (often persisting for months) and a rash — but it does not cause the degree of leucopenia and thrombocytopenia seen here. The severe retro-orbital pain and the specific laboratory findings (leucopenia, thrombocytopenia) are characteristic of dengue, not chikungunya. NSAIDs are contraindicated in dengue due to platelet dysfunction.',
'Incorrect. Typhoid presents with step-ladder fever, relative bradycardia, rose spots, and leucopenia — but not the retro-orbital pain, severe myalgia ("breakbone fever"), and thrombocytopenia seen here. The rash in typhoid (rose spots) is quite different from the dengue rash. Travel to Bali (dengue endemic) is far more consistent with dengue than typhoid.',
'Incorrect. Zika virus causes a mild febrile illness with rash, conjunctivitis, and arthralgia — usually self-limiting. It does not cause the severe thrombocytopenia, leucopenia, and systemic toxicity described here. While Zika is sexually transmissible and partner notification is important in pregnancy (due to microcephaly risk), the diagnosis here is dengue, not Zika.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q34 STREPTOCOCCAL PHARYNGITIS AND RHEUMATIC FEVER
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 10-year-old boy presents to his GP with severe sore throat and fever. His FeverPAIN score is 5. He is treated with penicillin V 250 mg QDS for 10 days. His mother asks why the full 10-day course is important rather than stopping when he feels better. According to your knowledge of Group A Streptococcal pharyngitis and its complications, which of the following most accurately explains the rationale for the full antibiotic course?',
'The 10-day course is required to clear the infection from the tonsils to prevent recurrence within the next 2 weeks',
'Completing the full 10-day course is important to eradicate Group A Streptococcus from the throat and prevent non-suppurative complications — specifically acute rheumatic fever (ARF) and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis; ARF can lead to rheumatic heart disease, which is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in young people worldwide',
'The 10-day course prevents spread of streptococcal infection to household contacts through airborne transmission',
'A shorter 5-day course is equally effective; the 10-day recommendation is outdated and based on pre-antibiotic era data',
'The 10-day course prevents development of antibiotic resistance in streptococcal strains',
'B',
'CORRECT. Acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is a delayed, non-suppurative inflammatory complication of Group A Streptococcal (GAS — Streptococcus pyogenes) pharyngeal infection. Pathophysiology: molecular mimicry — antibodies produced against GAS M-protein antigens cross-react with cardiac, joint, and brain tissue. ARF occurs 1–5 weeks after untreated or inadequately treated GAS pharyngitis. ARF manifestations (Jones Criteria): carditis (pancarditis — pericarditis, myocarditis, endocarditis — causing valve damage, particularly mitral), migratory polyarthritis, chorea (Sydenham''s), erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Rheumatic heart disease (RHD) — from recurrent ARF attacks — is the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children and young adults in developing countries, causing valvular damage (particularly mitral stenosis). Completing the full 10-day course of penicillin V eradicates GAS from the throat and prevents ARF. It also reduces infectivity within 24–48 hours of starting antibiotics. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is another non-suppurative complication (haematuria, oedema, hypertension) but is not prevented as reliably by antibiotics as ARF is. NICE',
'Incorrect. While completing the course reduces the risk of recurrence, the primary reason for a 10-day course of penicillin for streptococcal pharyngitis is to prevent non-suppurative complications — particularly acute rheumatic fever — not simply to prevent recurrence.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While reducing infectivity is a benefit of antibiotic treatment (GAS pharyngitis patients become non-infectious after 24–48 hours of antibiotics), this is not the primary reason for the full 10-day course. The main reason is to prevent ARF and RHD.',
'Incorrect. The 10-day course recommendation for GAS pharyngitis is well-supported by clinical evidence — it has been shown to be superior to shorter courses in eradicating GAS from the throat and reducing ARF risk. Shorter 5-day courses (used with some other antibiotics like azithromycin) have lower GAS eradication rates and are associated with higher recurrence rates.',
'Incorrect. Streptococcus pyogenes (GAS) remains universally sensitive to penicillin — it is one of the few bacteria that has shown essentially no acquired resistance to penicillin over decades of use. The concern about antibiotic resistance does not drive the recommendation for the 10-day penicillin course. The rationale is preventing ARF and RHD.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q35 MEASLES IN PRIMARY CARE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP receives a call from a mother whose 4-year-old daughter has developed a high fever (40°C), cough, coryza, conjunctivitis, and a red blotchy rash that started on her face and spread downward to her trunk and limbs over the past 2 days. On asking about vaccination history, the mother states the child has had no vaccinations as they declined immunisation. The child has not been seen in the surgery. According to NICE guidance on measles and public health management, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Advise the mother to bring the child to the waiting room immediately for examination',
'Advise the mother to keep the child away from the surgery and other public places; arrange a home visit or telephone/video consultation; if measles is confirmed clinically, notify the UKHSA health protection team immediately (measles is a notifiable disease); provide supportive care advice (antipyretics, fluids); identify close contacts who may need post-exposure prophylaxis',
'Prescribe oral antibiotics (amoxicillin) as secondary bacterial pneumonia is the expected complication in all measles cases',
'Reassure the mother that this is roseola infantum (sixth disease) as it presents identically to measles and is benign',
'Advise the mother to take the child to A&E immediately as measles always requires hospital admission',
'B',
'CORRECT. Measles is highly infectious (R0 of 12–18 — one of the most contagious infectious diseases known). Key management principles: (1) DO NOT bring the child to the surgery waiting room — measles is airborne and can infect susceptible contacts for hours after the infected patient has left a room. Arrange home visit or telephone/video consultation to avoid nosocomial spread in the surgery; (2) Clinical diagnosis: measles presents with the prodromal phase (3–5 days of fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis) followed by the characteristic morbilliform rash spreading centrifugally from the head downward. Koplik spots (white spots on buccal mucosa opposite the lower molars) are pathognomonic but transient; (3) Notification: measles is a notifiable disease — notify UKHSA health protection team immediately on clinical suspicion; (4) Contact tracing: identify susceptible contacts (unvaccinated, immunocompromised, pregnant women) — they may qualify for post-exposure prophylaxis (MMR vaccine within 72 hours OR human normal immunoglobulin within 6 days); (5) Exclusion from school/nursery for 4 days after rash onset. NICE/UKHSA',
'Incorrect. Bringing a child with suspected measles to the waiting room would expose other patients (including vulnerable groups — immunocompromised patients, unvaccinated infants, pregnant women) to one of the most contagious infections known. This is an infection control emergency that requires isolation, not attendance at the surgery.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Antibiotics are not routinely prescribed for all measles cases — most cases are viral and self-limiting. Antibiotics are indicated if there is evidence of secondary bacterial complication (otitis media, pneumonia, sinusitis). Empirical antibiotics for uncomplicated measles contribute to antimicrobial resistance without clinical benefit.',
'Incorrect. Roseola infantum (sixth disease — HHV-6 infection) presents differently: it causes 3–5 days of high fever that RESOLVES before the rash appears (the rash appears as the fever breaks — rose-pink, trunk-predominant, spreads peripherally). Measles has a prodromal phase where fever and cough/coryza/conjunctivitis COEXIST with the rash which starts on the face. The unvaccinated status, conjunctivitis, coryza, and centrifugal spread strongly suggest measles over roseola.',
'Incorrect. Most children with uncomplicated measles can be managed at home with supportive care. Hospital admission is required for: complications (measles encephalitis, measles pneumonitis, severe dehydration, secondary bacterial pneumonia in a seriously ill child, immunocompromised patients). Routine hospital admission for all measles cases is not appropriate.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q36 RESPIRATORY SYNCYTIAL VIRUS (RSV)
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A first-time mother brings her 8-week-old baby boy to the GP in December. He was born at 34 weeks gestation and was in the neonatal unit for 3 weeks. He has had increasing respiratory symptoms for 2 days: nasal congestion, cough, and difficulty feeding. On examination: respiratory rate 62/min, subcostal and intercostal recessions, SpO2 92% on air, widespread bilateral fine crackles and wheeze. He is alert. His temperature is 37.5°C. His weight is tracking on the 9th centile. According to NICE guidance on bronchiolitis in children, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe salbutamol 100 mcg 2 puffs via spacer every 4 hours as bronchodilators are first-line for bronchiolitis',
'Prescribe oral amoxicillin 125 mg TDS for 5 days as bronchiolitis commonly has a secondary bacterial component',
'Prescribe oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg OD for 3 days as steroids reduce the duration and severity of bronchiolitis',
'Call 999 for immediate emergency transfer — this premature infant has bronchiolitis with SpO2 92%, recession, and tachypnoea (RR 62), meeting criteria for urgent hospital admission; supplemental oxygen and possible nasogastric or IV feeding will be required',
'Reassure the mother that RSV bronchiolitis is self-limiting and advise saline nasal drops and upright positioning at home',
'D',
'CORRECT. NICE guidance on bronchiolitis identifies the following criteria for hospital admission (any one): SpO2 <92%, RR >60/min, moderate to severe respiratory distress (recession, nasal flaring, grunting), poor feeding (<50–75% normal intake), apnoeas, cyanosis, age <3 months, or high-risk features (prematurity, chronic lung disease of prematurity, congenital heart disease, immunocompromise). This infant meets MULTIPLE criteria: SpO2 92% (borderline at threshold), RR 62/min (>60), subcostal/intercostal recessions, and is ex-premature (34 weeks — high risk). Emergency admission is mandatory. Hospital management: supplemental oxygen to maintain SpO2 ≥92%, nasogastric or IV feeding if unable to maintain adequate oral intake, monitoring for apnoea (high risk in ex-premature infants). NICE guidance explicitly states: salbutamol, steroids, antibiotics, and adrenaline nebulisers are NOT recommended for bronchiolitis (no evidence of benefit). NICE',
'Incorrect. NICE guidance on bronchiolitis explicitly recommends AGAINST the routine use of bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium) in bronchiolitis — randomised trials show no clinical benefit in terms of oxygen saturation, duration of illness, or hospital admission rates. Bronchodilators should not be prescribed for bronchiolitis.',
'Incorrect. Bronchiolitis is primarily a viral infection (RSV in approximately 75% of cases). Secondary bacterial co-infection is uncommon. NICE guidance does not recommend routine antibiotics for bronchiolitis — they should only be prescribed if there is evidence of secondary bacterial infection (bacterial pneumonia, otitis media, UTI identified on investigation).',
'Incorrect. Corticosteroids (systemic or inhaled) are explicitly NOT recommended for bronchiolitis by NICE. Multiple large RCTs have demonstrated no benefit of corticosteroids in bronchiolitis — they do not reduce duration, severity, or hospitalisation rates. Prescribing prednisolone for bronchiolitis is against evidence-based guidelines.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While saline nasal drops and upright positioning are appropriate supportive measures for mild bronchiolitis managed at home, this infant has SpO2 92%, RR 62/min, recession, and ex-premature status — he meets multiple criteria for hospital admission per NICE. Home management would be dangerous for this infant with significant respiratory compromise.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q37 GIARDIASIS
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 35-year-old man presents to his GP with a 6-week history of loose, offensive, greasy stools that float and are difficult to flush (steatorrhoea), marked bloating, excessive flatulence, and mild abdominal cramping. He returned from a 3-week backpacking trip to Nepal 2 months ago. He has lost 4 kg. His stool microscopy identifies Giardia intestinalis cysts. His HIV test is negative. According to NICE guidance and BSG guidelines on giardiasis, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD for 5 days as giardiasis is a bacterial infection treated with fluoroquinolones',
'Prescribe oral metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 5–7 days as first-line treatment for Giardia infection; advise strict hygiene measures to prevent household transmission; notify the UKHSA if the patient is a food handler',
'Prescribe oral tinidazole 2 g as a single dose — it is equivalent to metronidazole but requires only one dose improving adherence',
'Reassure the patient that giardiasis is self-limiting and will resolve without treatment within 8 weeks',
'Prescribe oral albendazole 400 mg OD for 5 days as it is the most effective agent for Giardia',
'B',
'CORRECT. Giardia intestinalis (also called G. lamblia or G. duodenalis) is a flagellate protozoan parasite causing giardiasis — the most common intestinal parasitic infection in the UK and worldwide. Classic presentation: subacute/chronic diarrhoea with steatorrhoea (fatty, malodorous, floating stools), bloating, flatulence, and weight loss — developing 1–3 weeks after exposure. Transmission: faecal-oral route (contaminated water — particularly common in mountainous regions like Nepal where glacial meltwater contaminates streams). Treatment per NICE/BSG: (1) First-line: oral metronidazole 400 mg TDS for 5–7 days (effective in >90% of cases); (2) Alternative: tinidazole 2 g as a single dose (equivalent efficacy, better adherence) or nitazoxanide (where available); (3) Hygiene: avoid swimming in pools until stools are normal (highly chlorine-resistant), strict handwashing, exclude from food handling; (4) Notification: giardiasis is notifiable — particularly important if the patient is a food handler. NICE',
'Incorrect. Giardia intestinalis is a protozoan parasite — it is not a bacterium and does not respond to fluoroquinolone antibiotics (which target bacterial DNA gyrase). Ciprofloxacin has no antiparasitic activity against Giardia.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Tinidazole 2 g as a single dose is a valid alternative to metronidazole for giardiasis — it has equivalent efficacy and better adherence due to single-dose administration. However, it is listed as an alternative in NICE/BSG guidance, not the first-line recommendation. Metronidazole is first-line in the UK. The statement about tinidazole''s efficacy is correct but it is not the first-line choice.',
'Incorrect. Giardiasis is NOT reliably self-limiting in most adult patients — the subacute malabsorptive phase can persist for months, causing progressive weight loss and nutritional deficiencies. Treatment is indicated for all confirmed cases of symptomatic giardiasis. Watchful waiting for 8 weeks is not appropriate management.',
'Incorrect. Albendazole is used for helminths (intestinal worms — roundworm, hookworm, whipworm, tapeworm) and has some activity against Giardia in studies, but it is not recommended as first-line in NICE or BSG guidelines for giardiasis in the UK. Metronidazole remains the evidence-based first-line treatment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q38 SEXUALLY TRANSMITTED INFECTIONS — CHLAMYDIA
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 22-year-old woman presents to her GP after a positive chlamydia test result from the National Chlamydia Screening Programme. She tested via a self-taken vulvovaginal swab and the result was positive for Chlamydia trachomatis by NAAT testing. She is asymptomatic. She has a new boyfriend of 3 months and has had 2 previous partners in the past year. She is on the combined oral contraceptive pill. According to NICE guidance and BASHH guidelines on chlamydia management, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Arrange confirmatory repeat testing before treating as a single NAAT positive is insufficient for diagnosis',
'Prescribe oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days as the recommended first-line treatment for uncomplicated genital chlamydia; offer partner notification — all sexual contacts within the past 6 months should be tested and treated; advise no sexual intercourse until both patient and all partners have completed treatment',
'Prescribe oral azithromycin 1 g as a single dose as this is first-line for chlamydia and simplifies adherence',
'Refer to a sexual health clinic before treating as chlamydia in a young woman always requires specialist management',
'Prescribe oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days and advise she can resume sexual intercourse after taking the first dose',
'B',
'CORRECT. BASHH and NICE guidelines on chlamydia management (updated guidance): (1) Diagnosis: a single positive NAAT (nucleic acid amplification test) from a validated sample is sufficient to confirm the diagnosis — confirmatory repeat testing is NOT routinely recommended (option A is incorrect); (2) Treatment: BASHH 2018 updated guidance recommends oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days as first-line treatment for uncomplicated genital chlamydia in non-pregnant women, in preference to azithromycin 1 g stat (option C). Reason: studies showed higher treatment failure rates with azithromycin single dose compared to doxycycline 7 days; azithromycin remains appropriate for pregnant women (where doxycycline is contraindicated); (3) Partner notification: contacts from the past 6 months should be informed, tested, and treated; (4) Test of cure: not routinely recommended for uncomplicated genital chlamydia; repeat test at 3–6 months recommended due to high re-infection rates; (5) Abstinence from sexual intercourse: until both patient and all partners have completed treatment (not just after first dose). BASHH/NICE',
'Incorrect. A single positive NAAT is diagnostic for chlamydia — repeat confirmatory testing is not required before treatment. NAAT tests have high sensitivity (>90%) and specificity (>99%) from validated genital samples. Delaying treatment for a repeat test introduces unnecessary risk of ongoing transmission and potential complications (PID, ectopic pregnancy, infertility).',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Azithromycin 1 g stat was previously first-line for chlamydia but BASHH updated its guidance to recommend doxycycline 7 days as first-line for uncomplicated genital chlamydia following evidence of higher treatment failure rates with azithromycin. Azithromycin remains appropriate for pregnant women (doxycycline is contraindicated in pregnancy) and for those who cannot take doxycycline.',
'Incorrect. Uncomplicated genital chlamydia in a young woman is not a specialist-only condition — it is routinely managed in primary care and community sexual health services. Routine specialist referral before treating would be an inappropriate use of resources and would delay treatment unnecessarily.',
'Incorrect. Advising resumption of sexual intercourse after just one dose is incorrect — the patient should avoid intercourse until both she AND all her sexual partners have completed their full treatment course. Re-exposure to an untreated partner before treatment completion results in reinfection.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q39 HIV AND ANTIRETROVIRAL THERAPY IN PRIMARY CARE
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A 38-year-old man living with HIV is reviewed by his GP as part of shared care with the HIV clinic. He has been on tenofovir/emtricitabine/efavirenz for 5 years and has an undetectable viral load (HIV RNA <50 copies/mL) with a CD4 count of 680 cells/µL. He is otherwise well and has no side effect complaints. His most recent renal function tests show: eGFR 68 mL/min (down from 84 mL/min 2 years ago), creatinine 108 µmol/L (previously 88 µmol/L), phosphate 0.62 mmol/L (low — normal 0.8–1.5 mmol/L), and urine dipstick shows 1+ protein and 1+ glucose (despite normal blood glucose). According to your knowledge of tenofovir-associated nephrotoxicity, which of the following is the most appropriate action?',
'Continue the current regimen as eGFR 68 is within normal limits and no dose adjustment is required',
'Prescribe phosphate supplements to correct the low phosphate and continue tenofovir',
'The findings indicate tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)-associated proximal renal tubular toxicity (Fanconi syndrome) — alert the HIV clinic urgently as the antiretroviral regimen should be reviewed for substitution of TDF with tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) or an alternative; monitor renal function and phosphate closely',
'Stop all antiretroviral therapy immediately as continuing is causing kidney damage',
'Prescribe atorvastatin for the proteinuria as this is likely hypertensive nephropathy rather than drug toxicity',
'C',
'CORRECT. Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF — an older tenofovir formulation) is associated with renal tubular toxicity affecting the proximal convoluted tubule. The pattern of findings here is classic for TDF-associated proximal renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome): (1) Progressive eGFR decline (84→68 over 2 years); (2) Hypophosphataemia (0.62 mmol/L — phosphate wasting from proximal tubular dysfunction); (3) Glycosuria with normal blood glucose (glucose reabsorption impaired in proximal tubule); (4) Low-level proteinuria (tubular proteinuria). This constellation of tubular dysfunction findings in a patient on TDF indicates nephrotoxicity requiring urgent antiretroviral regimen review. The HIV clinic must be informed urgently. Tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) — a newer tenofovir prodrug — has equivalent antiviral efficacy with significantly lower renal and bone toxicity, and is the preferred substitution. Stopping all ARVs abruptly (option D) would cause viral rebound and is never appropriate. NICE/BHIVA HIV guidelines',
'Incorrect. eGFR 68 is within the normal range by eGFR standards (>60 mL/min is normal), but the progressive decline combined with hypophosphataemia, glycosuria with euglycaemia, and proteinuria points to TDF-associated Fanconi syndrome — a pattern of proximal tubular dysfunction. The eGFR trend and tubular markers matter more than the absolute eGFR value here.',
'Incorrect. Prescribing phosphate supplements addresses the biochemical abnormality but does not address the underlying cause — ongoing TDF-mediated proximal tubular injury. Without removing the causative agent, tubular damage will continue and may progress to significant CKD. The underlying nephrotoxicity must be addressed by substituting TDF.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Stopping all antiretroviral therapy abruptly would cause rapid viral rebound (exponential increase in HIV RNA), profound immunosuppression (CD4 decline), and risk of AIDS-defining illness. ARVs are never abruptly discontinued — the specific nephrotoxic agent (TDF) needs to be substituted with a safer alternative (TAF or another regimen), which is a decision for the HIV specialist team.',
'Incorrect. Atorvastatin reduces proteinuria in some settings (particularly diabetic nephropathy) but the proteinuria here is tubular in origin (part of Fanconi syndrome from TDF toxicity) — not glomerular proteinuria from hypertensive nephropathy. There is no blood pressure abnormality mentioned. Prescribing a statin does not address the underlying TDF nephrotoxicity.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q40 INFECTION PREVENTION — NEEDLESTICK
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Infectious Disease',NULL,'Hard',
'A practice nurse in a GP surgery attends occupational health after sustaining a needlestick injury from a hollow-bore needle. The needle had been used on a patient who is known to be HIV-positive with a viral load of 2,400 copies/mL (not undetectable). The injury occurred 45 minutes ago. The nurse is not HIV-positive, is hepatitis B-vaccinated (anti-HBs 120 mIU/mL), and has not taken any antiretroviral medication. According to PHE guidance on HIV post-exposure prophylaxis for healthcare workers, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?',
'No action is needed as HIV transmission from a needlestick with an HIV-positive patient is extremely rare',
'Prescribe 28 days of HIV post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) immediately — first dose within 1 hour of exposure ideally; the starter pack of tenofovir/emtricitabine + raltegravir (or equivalent) should be available in the GP surgery or nearest A&E; also assess hepatitis C status of source patient',
'Arrange urgent HIV testing for the nurse before starting PEP as she may already be HIV-positive',
'Administer hepatitis B immunoglobulin as the nurse is at risk from both HIV and hepatitis B from this needlestick',
'Refer to the occupational health department for assessment in 48 hours before initiating any treatment',
'B',
'CORRECT. HIV post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) is highly effective at preventing HIV transmission after occupational exposure if started promptly. The risk of HIV transmission from a hollow-bore needlestick from an HIV-positive source with detectable viraemia (2,400 copies/mL) is approximately 1 in 300 (0.3%) — this is sufficient to warrant PEP. PHE guidance on occupational PEP: (1) PEP must be started AS SOON AS POSSIBLE — ideally within 1 hour, maximum benefit within 72 hours (after 72 hours, PEP is not recommended); (2) Recommended regimen: tenofovir/emtricitabine (Truvada or generic) + raltegravir 400 mg BD for 28 days (or equivalent 3-drug regimen per local protocol); (3) Starter packs should be available in occupational health departments or A&E; (4) Follow-up: HIV test at baseline, 6 weeks, 12 weeks post-exposure; hepatitis C baseline and 12-week test; (5) Hepatitis B: nurse has adequate protective anti-HBs (120 mIU/mL >10 mIU/mL) — HBIg not required; (6) Report to occupational health for formal documentation. PHE/BHIVA PEP guidelines',
'Incorrect. While the individual risk per needlestick is approximately 0.3%, this is sufficient to justify PEP when the source has detectable HIV viraemia and the exposure is significant (hollow-bore needle). An undetectable viral load (<50 copies/mL) in the source significantly reduces but does not eliminate risk — and in this case, the viral load IS detectable at 2,400 copies/mL. PEP should be initiated.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While baseline HIV testing of the healthcare worker is part of the PEP protocol, it should NOT delay starting PEP. A baseline HIV test is performed to exclude pre-existing HIV infection (to distinguish seroconversion from PEP failure) — but PEP should begin immediately without waiting for results. Waiting for results before starting PEP loses precious time.',
'Incorrect. The nurse has documented adequate hepatitis B immunity (anti-HBs 120 mIU/mL — well above the 10 mIU/mL protective threshold). Hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIg) is indicated for unvaccinated or non-immune individuals exposed to hepatitis B — not for those with proven immunity. HBIg would be unnecessary and wasteful in this case.',
'Incorrect. Waiting 48 hours for occupational health assessment before initiating PEP is completely unacceptable — after 72 hours, PEP is not recommended, and its efficacy decreases significantly with every passing hour. PEP must be initiated immediately (within 1 hour ideally) through A&E if occupational health is not immediately available. The 48-hour referral window is far too late.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0);

